WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.636
- Look what they're offering us now?

00:00:01.636 --> 00:00:04.780
Their answer is, "Okay we've
removed God from the equation

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:05.613
and what do we get?"

00:00:05.613 --> 00:00:06.470
We get government.

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:09.139
And they now pray basically to government.

00:00:09.139 --> 00:00:11.722
(upbeat music)

00:00:17.617 --> 00:00:18.773
(audience applauding)

00:00:18.773 --> 00:00:21.493
- Wow, thank you very much.

00:00:21.493 --> 00:00:22.990
That is a great welcome.

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:23.823
Thank you so much.

00:00:23.823 --> 00:00:26.320
Well welcome tonight's "Big Conversation,"

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:30.157
filmed live here in
Costa Mesa, California.

00:00:30.157 --> 00:00:31.790
"The Big Conversation" is produced

00:00:31.790 --> 00:00:33.930
by Premier Christian Radio in partnership

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:35.900
with the Templeton Religion Trust

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:38.040
and it's a series of video discussions

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.420
between thinkers across
the religious spectrum,

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:42.710
looking at some of the
biggest questions in life.

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:43.550
Who are we?

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:44.960
What's it all about?

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:46.840
Looking at science, faith, philosophy,

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:48.440
what it means to be human.

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:50.990
I'm delighted to say that tonight

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:53.870
we're going to be sitting down
and hearing from two people

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:55.830
I've really been looking forward

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:57.960
to bringing into conversation together.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:02.700
We're gonna be looking at
the question of is God dead?

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:06.770
It's a conversation on faith,
culture and the modern world.

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:08.630
Where are the next generation turning to

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:12.417
in an increasingly post-Christian society?

00:01:12.417 --> 00:01:15.350
"Is God dead," as Nietzsche once declared?

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:19.070
Or is there space for a
renaissance of religious belief

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:20.243
in the modern world?

00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:25.260
John Lennox, who is here on my
left, is emeritus professor.

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:27.010
Yeah give him a round of applause.

00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:32.010
(audience applauding)
(audience cheering)

00:01:34.893 --> 00:01:36.280
Just to give a brief info,

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:38.820
John is emeritus professor of mathematics

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:41.690
and the philosophy of
science at Oxford University.

00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:43.160
He's a leading Christian thinker

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:44.150
and he's engaged many

00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:47.090
of the world's leading
atheist voices as well.

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:48.297
His latest book is called,

00:01:48.297 --> 00:01:50.730
"Can Science Explain Everything?"

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:53.460
And it is available as well after.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:54.700
Dave Rubin is my other guest.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:55.753
Let's have a round of applause for Dave.

00:01:55.753 --> 00:01:58.920
(audience applauding)

00:02:04.067 --> 00:02:06.230
Now Dave hosts the "Rubin Report."

00:02:06.230 --> 00:02:07.710
It's an online talk show

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:10.478
that reaches millions
of people every week.

00:02:10.478 --> 00:02:14.040
Dave is gonna be telling us
about his religious background

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:15.500
and where he's at now.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:17.850
So I won't sort of label him at this point

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:21.140
but he regularly hosts conversations

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:24.910
with leading cultural and
religious thinkers on his show.

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:25.942
So I'm really looking forward to

00:02:25.942 --> 00:02:27.990
and he's going to be bringing
to this conversation today.

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:31.297
He has a new book out it's
available for pre-order,

00:02:31.297 --> 00:02:34.327
"Don't Burn This Book," is the title.

00:02:34.327 --> 00:02:35.470
(audience laughing)

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:37.380
Thinking for yourself
in an age of unreason.

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:39.340
So I do recommend you go and get that

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:41.500
if you can on pre-order as well.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:43.820
So one more time just
please give a warm round

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:47.213
of applause to my guests,
John Lennox and Dave Rubin.

00:02:47.213 --> 00:02:52.213
(audience applauding)
(audience cheering)

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:59.580
Well, welcome gentlemen
both tonight's discussion.

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:01.520
I'm really looking forward

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:02.970
to what we're gonna be covering tonight

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:06.030
because I've had you on
the show before, John,

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:08.290
and very often I've
put you in conversation

00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:11.480
with maybe a quite firm atheist,

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:14.783
someone like a Dawkins or
an Atkins or a Michael Ruse.

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:17.800
But I think tonight's gonna
be a little bit different

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:18.840
because I'm really looking forward

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:20.390
to hearing your story, Dave.

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:23.800
We haven't met before, in fact,
tonight was the first time

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:26.290
but I've watched your stuff and
I've seen some of the people

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:28.690
that you've increasingly been talking to

00:03:28.690 --> 00:03:30.010
and being influenced yourself

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:33.250
and it'll be fascinating
to hear where you're at now

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:34.630
in terms of both the cultural

00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:37.450
and religious aspects of your worldview.

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:39.420
- Well, I've never had
anyone give me an intro

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:41.198
and say we'll label him later.

00:03:41.198 --> 00:03:43.470
(audience laughing)

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:47.140
So I'm feeling really
undue pressure right now

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:49.315
and I'm the only one here from SoCal,

00:03:49.315 --> 00:03:51.430
so let's see what happens.

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:54.260
- Well, whatever those labels may be,

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:55.380
I'm really looking forward

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:57.500
to what you're gonna bring to us tonight.

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:00.380
Perhaps we'll start with
you first of all, Dave.

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:02.613
Tell us a little bit about
who you are, growing up.

00:04:02.613 --> 00:04:07.147
I know that you came from
a pretty religious family.

00:04:07.147 --> 00:04:08.097
So do you want to tell
a little bit about that

00:04:08.097 --> 00:04:10.377
and where you found
yourself as you grew up

00:04:10.377 --> 00:04:13.000
and as regards sort of faith and so on.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:14.390
- Sure, first off I just want to say

00:04:14.390 --> 00:04:15.980
what a pleasure it is to be here.

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:18.940
You know, I'm usually on the
other side of the interview

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:22.210
and the reason I'm particularly
excited about tonight is

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:24.160
that I don't talk about this sort of thing

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:25.830
from a personal perspective that often.

00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:29.580
You know I've sat down
with tons of atheists,

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:31.130
Sam Harris and Michael Shermer

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:32.890
and Peter Boghossian and that whole crew.

00:04:32.890 --> 00:04:35.480
And I've talked to
plenty of people of faith

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:37.280
like Bishop Aaron and Rabbi Wolpe

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:39.163
and plenty of other people that come

00:04:39.163 --> 00:04:41.350
from different political perspectives

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:43.490
and personal perspectives.

00:04:43.490 --> 00:04:48.340
And I always find that
I go into each interview

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:51.350
with no agenda other than
hearing their thoughts

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:54.450
and seeing how that shakes
out around my worldview.

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:56.580
That being said, I'm excited to be here

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:58.580
because I can sort of
tell you a little bit more

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:01.220
about where I come from
and sort of where I'm at.

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:04.960
So I grew up in a conservative
Jewish household in New York.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:06.380
We kept kosher.

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:10.413
We did Shabbat on Friday
nights, all the big holidays,

00:05:11.888 --> 00:05:14.360
but there was a strong
secular belief within that.

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:16.510
And as I was telling
you sort of backstage,

00:05:18.425 --> 00:05:21.700
you know there's an interesting
piece related to Judaism

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:23.840
that I think is a little
different than other religions

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:27.930
in that the ethnic tie to
it, at least in a modern way,

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:30.400
is for most Jews more important

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:33.840
than the religious nature
of it, specifically.

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:36.000
Let's say beliefs specifically.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:37.420
Because John, as we were talking about,

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:39.730
there are many, many Jews,

00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:42.800
especially in sciences and in mathematics,

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:45.170
that aren't believers per se

00:05:45.170 --> 00:05:46.810
but have a real cultural affinity

00:05:46.810 --> 00:05:50.160
and I would say that
that's sort of where I'm at

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:51.990
or at least where I've been
over the last couple years.

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:54.670
I actually am now in the last few years

00:05:54.670 --> 00:05:57.360
and this has to do a
lot with being on tour

00:05:57.360 --> 00:05:59.320
with Jordan Peterson for a year.

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:02.730
Jordan and I did about 110
stops in one calendar year

00:06:02.730 --> 00:06:04.050
in about 20 countries.

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:05.170
It was pretty amazing.

00:06:05.170 --> 00:06:10.120
And when you spend that
kind of time listening

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:12.730
to a true innovative thinker,

00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:15.340
I mean truly the guy that I think is

00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:20.053
the world's most important
public philosopher, let's say.

00:06:20.950 --> 00:06:23.110
You know, talking about
his biblical lectures

00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:24.850
and talking about his perspective on life

00:06:24.850 --> 00:06:29.210
and that there has to be
a bedrock of something

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:33.570
that is real and true outside of us

00:06:34.420 --> 00:06:37.453
and then how he relates that
through the biblical stories.

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:42.520
it moved me, it moved me
over the course of the year

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:44.100
that we did this together.

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:49.100
So I would say I'm secular
basically in my life

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:54.050
but I definitely in the
last year have found

00:06:54.050 --> 00:06:56.870
that there has to be
something outside of us.

00:06:56.870 --> 00:06:58.160
The rest of this makes no sense.

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:00.710
I mean the part, very briefly, the part

00:07:00.710 --> 00:07:03.370
that you know I'm really known
for is the political part

00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:06.500
and that I was lefty and the
difference between leftism.

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:08.151
- [Justin] You kind of
had a political conversion

00:07:08.151 --> 00:07:08.984
of sorts, yeah.
- I had, right.

00:07:08.984 --> 00:07:10.420
So I'm usually much
more comfortable talking

00:07:10.420 --> 00:07:15.210
about my political conversion
than a religious one.

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:16.910
But I would say this.

00:07:16.910 --> 00:07:19.100
That consistent with me talking

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:22.160
about sort of what's happened
with the postmodern left

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:24.190
with the progressives and we see this now

00:07:24.190 --> 00:07:27.610
where there's sort of nothing
that's empirically true

00:07:27.610 --> 00:07:30.470
and any given day you can feel anything

00:07:30.470 --> 00:07:34.610
about any particular topic,
there's a reason for that.

00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:37.810
And the reason is they've
disconnected everything.

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:40.630
Their whole worldview is disconnected

00:07:40.630 --> 00:07:42.520
to anything that came before them

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:45.610
so that that could be God
or a religious set of ideas

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:46.620
or something like that.

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:49.950
So I'm really, really
fascinated by that at the moment

00:07:49.950 --> 00:07:52.780
and it's changing how I live my life.

00:07:52.780 --> 00:07:54.290
I just did, it was Yom Kippur,

00:07:54.290 --> 00:07:55.820
which is the holiest day in Judaism.

00:07:55.820 --> 00:07:58.660
I was at a service that
was actually at a church

00:07:58.660 --> 00:08:02.290
in Pasadena in Los Angeles
hosted by Dennis Prager

00:08:02.290 --> 00:08:04.290
that I'm sure many of you guys know about,

00:08:04.290 --> 00:08:05.610
Dennis Prager, who many of you know.

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:09.280
So I'm sort of, I would say I'm in it

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:10.670
the way you guys are all in it,

00:08:10.670 --> 00:08:12.680
trying to find some truth in the madness.

00:08:12.680 --> 00:08:14.280
- I'll be interested to tease that out

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:15.370
a little more in due course.

00:08:15.370 --> 00:08:17.550
I mean one thing I did notice is

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:20.162
I have seen just watching
some of your videos

00:08:20.162 --> 00:08:21.430
that there definitely been a progression

00:08:21.430 --> 00:08:23.910
in your thinking on this.

00:08:23.910 --> 00:08:25.280
And probably if you go back a few years,

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:27.010
I think you had said,

00:08:27.010 --> 00:08:28.350
along the lines of you probably thought

00:08:28.350 --> 00:08:29.370
of yourself as an atheist.

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:31.770
But evidently that's not
quite the case anymore.

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:35.000
- Well I had a bunch of
atheists, high-profile atheists,

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:36.930
on the show in a row.

00:08:36.930 --> 00:08:38.250
Starting with Sam Harris,

00:08:38.250 --> 00:08:41.350
who I admire and he's
a good friend of mine

00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:42.990
and Michael Shermer and Peter Boghossian.

00:08:42.990 --> 00:08:46.490
And I really love the
intellectual side of that.

00:08:46.490 --> 00:08:48.490
I really, really do love it

00:08:48.490 --> 00:08:52.270
and that's not at the exclusion
of anything else actually.

00:08:52.270 --> 00:08:53.103
But what I found was

00:08:53.103 --> 00:08:55.080
that I had had a series
of atheists on in a row

00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:57.420
and then people online just kept saying

00:08:57.420 --> 00:08:58.544
that I was an atheist.

00:08:58.544 --> 00:08:59.377
- Right.

00:08:59.377 --> 00:09:03.043
- And then I sort of just
said it one day without,

00:09:04.310 --> 00:09:07.290
it didn't mean anything to me
sort of one way or another.

00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:09.010
It was almost, it just sort of came,

00:09:09.010 --> 00:09:10.840
it just sort of came
out of my mouth one day.

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:12.740
And then two years ago,

00:09:12.740 --> 00:09:14.260
you know I do this
off-the-grid August thing

00:09:14.260 --> 00:09:16.630
where I literally lock my phone in a safe

00:09:16.630 --> 00:09:19.210
and I don't look at
any news or television.

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:21.790
I am completely offline
and I really disappear

00:09:21.790 --> 00:09:23.590
and I try to let my brain reset.

00:09:23.590 --> 00:09:26.777
And two years ago when I did it,

00:09:26.777 --> 00:09:29.180
one of the thoughts that
I kept having sort of

00:09:29.180 --> 00:09:33.120
in my peace was that I'm not an atheist.

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:35.407
And I came back and I said
it in a very casual way.

00:09:35.407 --> 00:09:37.230
And I just did this live stream

00:09:37.230 --> 00:09:39.000
where I just sort of
said it very flippantly

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:40.440
that I just don't like the word atheist,

00:09:40.440 --> 00:09:42.570
it doesn't fit what I believe.

00:09:42.570 --> 00:09:43.820
I do believe in something else

00:09:43.820 --> 00:09:47.100
even if I can't completely
articulate what it is.

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:48.500
I think Jordan has gone a long way

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:51.143
to articulating the type
of thing that I believe in.

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:55.170
And I got a lot of hate for that one

00:09:55.170 --> 00:09:57.540
because you know the atheists,

00:09:57.540 --> 00:09:59.730
they don't like a converted person either.

00:09:59.730 --> 00:10:03.360
So, you gotta watch out for that too.

00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:05.730
So you know we all have our own trappings.

00:10:05.730 --> 00:10:10.690
But what I'm most interested
in is talking to people

00:10:10.690 --> 00:10:12.490
from all walks of life and figuring out

00:10:12.490 --> 00:10:14.400
what the common stuff is.

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:16.610
And what I like to frame
that around is a conversation

00:10:16.610 --> 00:10:18.740
about freedom and how
do we limit government

00:10:18.740 --> 00:10:21.440
so we can all believe
what we want to believe

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:22.590
and think what we want to think

00:10:22.590 --> 00:10:25.560
and be part of a society
that's pluralistic

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:27.590
and decent for all of us.

00:10:27.590 --> 00:10:29.570
- John, let's have a bit
of an introduction to you

00:10:29.570 --> 00:10:31.870
for those who aren't familiar with you

00:10:31.870 --> 00:10:35.870
Tell us about your own faith
journey up to this point.

00:10:35.870 --> 00:10:37.930
You now obviously speak to many people

00:10:37.930 --> 00:10:39.860
all over the world about Christianity.

00:10:39.860 --> 00:10:42.371
But where did it all begin for you?

00:10:42.371 --> 00:10:44.430
- It began for me, and let me say as well,

00:10:44.430 --> 00:10:48.750
how delighted I am to
be back in Costa Mesa

00:10:48.750 --> 00:10:52.590
and I've enjoyed in the
past some marvelous shows

00:10:52.590 --> 00:10:56.610
with Justin and I'm just fascinated

00:10:56.610 --> 00:11:00.570
by what's gonna happen in
the conversation tonight.

00:11:00.570 --> 00:11:03.500
But I grew up in Northern Ireland

00:11:03.500 --> 00:11:06.877
which isn't always the best
start to discuss religion

00:11:09.160 --> 00:11:11.440
because it was a divided community

00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:15.140
and there was a lot of
terrorism that was connected

00:11:15.140 --> 00:11:19.050
in a very complex way to Christianity

00:11:19.050 --> 00:11:22.020
of both versions, Protestant and Catholic.

00:11:22.020 --> 00:11:24.130
But the important thing was

00:11:24.130 --> 00:11:27.520
that my parents were very unusual

00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:30.740
for that kind of cultural context.

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:34.220
They were Christian, convinced Christians,

00:11:34.220 --> 00:11:38.670
but they weren't sectarian
and that was very unusual.

00:11:38.670 --> 00:11:41.120
My father had a small business.

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:45.250
We lived in a small town, 15,000 or so

00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:47.200
and he tried to employ people

00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:49.090
from both sides of the community.

00:11:49.090 --> 00:11:51.020
Now why did he do that?

00:11:51.020 --> 00:11:52.060
I once asked him.

00:11:52.060 --> 00:11:54.520
I said, "Dad, it's so risky."

00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:56.490
And he was bombed for doing this.

00:11:56.490 --> 00:11:58.840
My brother nearly lost his life.

00:11:58.840 --> 00:12:00.687
And he said, "Look," he said,

00:12:00.687 --> 00:12:04.807
"I believe that every person,

00:12:04.807 --> 00:12:09.177
"whatever they believe,
is of infinite value

00:12:09.177 --> 00:12:11.147
"because they're made in the image of God,

00:12:11.147 --> 00:12:13.357
"going back to the Hebrew Scriptures

00:12:13.357 --> 00:12:17.480
"and therefore I will employ
across the community."

00:12:17.480 --> 00:12:20.163
And that has stuck with me.

00:12:20.163 --> 00:12:23.110
And it's been very important
when you're discussing,

00:12:23.110 --> 00:12:27.340
as I often do, with people
that do not share my worldview,

00:12:27.340 --> 00:12:28.960
that always comes to my mind.

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:31.130
Here's a person in front of me,

00:12:31.130 --> 00:12:33.900
and it relates to what you
were saying about freedom.

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:36.940
I would connect with freedom value

00:12:36.940 --> 00:12:40.660
that here's something
outside of my parents

00:12:40.660 --> 00:12:44.120
that gave every human
being dignity and value.

00:12:44.120 --> 00:12:45.710
That was point number one.

00:12:45.710 --> 00:12:50.710
The second thing was that
they allowed me to think.

00:12:51.290 --> 00:12:53.670
Now Northern Ireland's often associated

00:12:53.670 --> 00:12:57.580
with religious bigotry,
extreme fundamentalism,

00:12:57.580 --> 00:12:59.390
all this kind of thing.

00:12:59.390 --> 00:13:03.360
And my parents were not highly educated

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:05.500
but they really gave me space.

00:13:05.500 --> 00:13:08.120
So my first encounter
with Christianity was

00:13:08.120 --> 00:13:12.330
not mind closing, it was mind expanding.

00:13:12.330 --> 00:13:16.580
And I remember when I was about 13,

00:13:16.580 --> 00:13:18.027
my father came along, he says,

00:13:18.027 --> 00:13:20.100
"Here's a book you need to read."

00:13:20.100 --> 00:13:23.010
It was Marx's "Das Kapital."

00:13:23.010 --> 00:13:24.510
I said, "Dad, have you read it?"

00:13:24.510 --> 00:13:25.550
He said, "No."

00:13:25.550 --> 00:13:28.587
(audience laughing)

00:13:28.587 --> 00:13:30.267
"So why should I read it?"

00:13:30.267 --> 00:13:33.270
"You need to know what
other people think."

00:13:33.270 --> 00:13:34.400
I never forget that.

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:36.600
It was, set a compass bearing.

00:13:36.600 --> 00:13:40.640
The third point is their
Christianity was credible,

00:13:40.640 --> 00:13:45.210
morally credible, they actually
lived what they believed.

00:13:45.210 --> 00:13:49.220
So in that sense I had a
hugely privileged background

00:13:49.220 --> 00:13:54.220
that didn't compress me into
a narrow-minded bigoted person

00:13:54.780 --> 00:13:58.650
and it was noticeable when
I went to Cambridge in 1962.

00:13:58.650 --> 00:14:00.800
Not 1862, I know I look old.

00:14:00.800 --> 00:14:01.633
(audience laughing)

00:14:01.633 --> 00:14:05.040
But when I went to Cambridge in 1962,

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:07.460
many of my contemporaries from Ireland,

00:14:07.460 --> 00:14:09.110
the moment they got out of the country

00:14:09.110 --> 00:14:11.430
that was the end of any Christianity

00:14:11.430 --> 00:14:14.020
because they'd never made it their own.

00:14:14.020 --> 00:14:15.600
They'd never thought about it.

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:18.000
But I'd been encouraged to think about.

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:22.770
And that sort of set the compass bearings.

00:14:22.770 --> 00:14:26.470
There's one further point
that really shaped my life.

00:14:26.470 --> 00:14:30.350
I was challenged in
Cambridge, very early on,

00:14:30.350 --> 00:14:33.200
by student at table at night.

00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:36.270
And he said, asked, "Do
you believe in God?"

00:14:36.270 --> 00:14:38.367
And then he said, "Oh sorry, sorry, sorry.

00:14:38.367 --> 00:14:40.919
"I shouldn't have asked
you that, you're Irish.

00:14:40.919 --> 00:14:42.307
(audience laughing)

00:14:42.307 --> 00:14:45.684
"All you Irish believe in
God and you fight about it."

00:14:45.684 --> 00:14:49.310
And I'd heard that many times
but somehow it was different.

00:14:49.310 --> 00:14:52.160
And I thought, gosh yes, you know,

00:14:52.160 --> 00:14:53.820
I've never really met atheists.

00:14:53.820 --> 00:14:55.500
You know in Ireland people divided

00:14:55.500 --> 00:14:57.750
the Protestant atheist
and Catholic atheists.

00:14:59.927 --> 00:15:03.040
But they're not really real atheists.

00:15:03.040 --> 00:15:08.040
So I thought, what I'm going
to do is to start today

00:15:09.190 --> 00:15:13.300
and befriend people, befriend
them that's important,

00:15:13.300 --> 00:15:14.860
that do not share my worldview.

00:15:14.860 --> 00:15:17.950
And I've spent my whole life doing it.

00:15:17.950 --> 00:15:22.120
So that really sets the scene I think.

00:15:22.120 --> 00:15:26.110
- So Nietzsche famously
declared, "God is dead."

00:15:26.110 --> 00:15:28.510
Now he may have been a
bit premature in that

00:15:28.510 --> 00:15:31.150
but maybe he's finally, you know,

00:15:31.150 --> 00:15:34.480
his thoughts are coming
true in 21st century West

00:15:34.480 --> 00:15:35.330
because we are living

00:15:35.330 --> 00:15:39.670
in an increasingly
post-Christian age, people say.

00:15:39.670 --> 00:15:40.980
Increasingly the number of people

00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:43.610
who tick the census box that says none,

00:15:43.610 --> 00:15:46.270
no religious affiliation
is going up and so on.

00:15:46.270 --> 00:15:49.300
I mean you're engaging with
this kind of demographic

00:15:49.300 --> 00:15:51.783
all the time on your show, Dave.

00:15:52.840 --> 00:15:53.673
What's your feeling?

00:15:53.673 --> 00:15:58.400
Do you do feel like people are
genuinely less religious now?

00:15:58.400 --> 00:16:00.060
To what extent are some of those friends

00:16:00.060 --> 00:16:01.550
that you made early on in your show,

00:16:01.550 --> 00:16:02.770
people like Sam Harris

00:16:02.770 --> 00:16:05.320
and another well-known
atheists responsible

00:16:05.320 --> 00:16:08.720
for people moving away
from the religious bearings

00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:09.980
that they once had?

00:16:09.980 --> 00:16:12.440
- So obviously I don't
want to speak for Sam

00:16:12.440 --> 00:16:13.990
or any of those guys.

00:16:13.990 --> 00:16:15.960
What I have found in the conversations

00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:19.210
that I've had with non-believers
and with believers is

00:16:19.210 --> 00:16:22.590
that at a micro level
you can be a non-believer

00:16:22.590 --> 00:16:26.850
and be absolutely moral
and decent and good

00:16:26.850 --> 00:16:30.130
and a productive member of
society and all of those things

00:16:30.130 --> 00:16:33.180
as I believe those couple
people that I mentioned are.

00:16:33.180 --> 00:16:35.430
What I think is becoming the problem

00:16:35.430 --> 00:16:36.540
and I think this was really

00:16:36.540 --> 00:16:39.150
where Jordan Peterson hit on something is

00:16:39.150 --> 00:16:42.290
that societies can't organize around that.

00:16:42.290 --> 00:16:44.480
That it can sort of work for a while

00:16:44.480 --> 00:16:46.620
and there's you know most of
the things that I believe in

00:16:46.620 --> 00:16:49.580
and I talk about the
individual all the time

00:16:49.580 --> 00:16:51.980
and why I believe that
classical liberalism is

00:16:51.980 --> 00:16:54.330
the best sort of framework
for a political system

00:16:54.330 --> 00:16:55.330
that we should have.

00:16:56.440 --> 00:17:01.150
They almost can't exist without
that underlying bedrock.

00:17:01.150 --> 00:17:04.130
And so your question sort of gets

00:17:04.130 --> 00:17:05.170
to what I was saying earlier

00:17:05.170 --> 00:17:07.850
which is that the reason
that the secular world feels

00:17:07.850 --> 00:17:10.570
so out of control right
now, I mean just yesterday,

00:17:10.570 --> 00:17:12.580
I'm sure some of you guys saw that CNN did

00:17:12.580 --> 00:17:15.680
this quality Town Hall last night.

00:17:15.680 --> 00:17:17.040
And it was like you know everyone has

00:17:17.040 --> 00:17:19.150
to mention their gender pronouns

00:17:19.150 --> 00:17:23.070
and you have to admit that
there are more than two genders

00:17:23.070 --> 00:17:24.760
and all of these things

00:17:24.760 --> 00:17:28.030
that we know these
conversations are not being had.

00:17:28.030 --> 00:17:30.570
There are settled science debates

00:17:30.570 --> 00:17:34.370
that went on for a long time
that we know what facts are

00:17:34.370 --> 00:17:37.740
and yet we find because this
has now become untethered

00:17:37.740 --> 00:17:40.460
to anything other than how you feel

00:17:40.460 --> 00:17:42.390
that now everything is up for grabs.

00:17:42.390 --> 00:17:45.100
And that's why it sort of feels like

00:17:45.100 --> 00:17:47.790
that there's something sort
of godless happening here

00:17:47.790 --> 00:17:49.150
or something like that.

00:17:49.150 --> 00:17:52.100
Now trust me that is a hard thing

00:17:52.100 --> 00:17:54.390
for someone like me to say.

00:17:54.390 --> 00:17:58.440
As someone that really my
beliefs really are rooted

00:17:58.440 --> 00:18:01.140
in the Enlightenment and
the Enlightenment thinkers

00:18:01.140 --> 00:18:03.990
and this a real debate amongst people

00:18:03.990 --> 00:18:05.310
who talk about the Enlightenment.

00:18:05.310 --> 00:18:08.320
Could they have done it, could
they have reformed religions

00:18:08.320 --> 00:18:12.180
and burst liberalism in
a positive way forward

00:18:12.180 --> 00:18:15.260
without some religious belief behind it?

00:18:15.260 --> 00:18:17.000
I don't know the answer to that exactly,

00:18:17.000 --> 00:18:19.383
I don't know that we'll ever
really know the answer to that.

00:18:20.230 --> 00:18:23.730
But I would say that
the reason I first said

00:18:23.730 --> 00:18:25.490
that I'm happy to be here
with you guys is that

00:18:25.490 --> 00:18:30.200
in the last year where now
I've virtually only get invited

00:18:30.200 --> 00:18:33.000
to events by conservative groups

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:34.960
or libertarian groups for sure

00:18:34.960 --> 00:18:36.640
but groups on the right let's say

00:18:36.640 --> 00:18:38.650
but I often get invited to churches.

00:18:38.650 --> 00:18:41.000
I often get invited to places of faith.

00:18:41.000 --> 00:18:42.730
Now I know we can go through a litany

00:18:42.730 --> 00:18:45.540
of political disagreements
that we may or may not have

00:18:45.540 --> 00:18:47.900
and I absolutely know that everyone

00:18:47.900 --> 00:18:50.050
in this room would be happy to do that

00:18:50.050 --> 00:18:51.430
and there would be nobody fighting,

00:18:51.430 --> 00:18:52.780
there would be nobody screaming,

00:18:52.780 --> 00:18:55.710
we could explore those
ideas as far as we can

00:18:55.710 --> 00:18:57.090
and then we would put it down in front

00:18:57.090 --> 00:18:58.260
and either agree to disagree

00:18:58.260 --> 00:19:01.150
or maybe we'd move each
other one way or another

00:19:01.150 --> 00:19:03.030
and that would be wonderful.

00:19:03.030 --> 00:19:05.420
And I don't think that's a coincidence.

00:19:05.420 --> 00:19:08.970
I don't think it's a
coincidence that you guys here

00:19:09.860 --> 00:19:14.750
and that generally believers
right now are more tolerant.

00:19:14.750 --> 00:19:16.610
They are, it's just the reality.

00:19:16.610 --> 00:19:20.140
The people, yeah, give
yourselves a round of applause.

00:19:20.140 --> 00:19:21.590
(audience applauding)

00:19:21.590 --> 00:19:24.300
I mean that really is true.

00:19:24.300 --> 00:19:27.930
Who are the most intolerant
people in society right now?

00:19:27.930 --> 00:19:30.160
It's the people that are
constantly telling you

00:19:30.160 --> 00:19:31.553
how tolerant they are.

00:19:31.553 --> 00:19:32.610
(audience laughing)

00:19:32.610 --> 00:19:34.130
That's the irony.

00:19:34.130 --> 00:19:36.610
It's the people that tell
you you're a bunch of racists

00:19:36.610 --> 00:19:39.064
and bigots and homophobes
and the rest of it.

00:19:39.064 --> 00:19:42.070
And that's the real bizarre flip

00:19:42.070 --> 00:19:43.770
that we have happening in society.

00:19:43.770 --> 00:19:47.860
And I think that is linked to either,

00:19:47.860 --> 00:19:48.830
however you want to phrase it,

00:19:48.830 --> 00:19:50.270
either a post-Christian world

00:19:50.270 --> 00:19:53.550
or a post Judeo-Christian
world or a postmodern world,

00:19:53.550 --> 00:19:54.550
however you want to define that.

00:19:54.550 --> 00:19:55.790
- I mean to what extent do you agree

00:19:55.790 --> 00:19:57.950
with Dave's analysis
there of what's going on

00:19:57.950 --> 00:20:00.850
especially I suppose
at that academic level

00:20:00.850 --> 00:20:05.310
and in terms of the kind
of conversations now

00:20:05.310 --> 00:20:07.720
that you are and aren't
allowed to have almost

00:20:07.720 --> 00:20:09.053
when it comes to these issues?

00:20:09.053 --> 00:20:13.410
- Well, I think it's a
pretty accurate analysis

00:20:13.410 --> 00:20:17.180
and that's what I experienced right there.

00:20:17.180 --> 00:20:20.060
I'm always interested in
the phrase "God's dead,"

00:20:20.060 --> 00:20:22.749
because it seems to
assume he was alive once.

00:20:22.749 --> 00:20:24.340
(audience laughing)

00:20:24.340 --> 00:20:29.340
And of course I hear Richard
Dawkins kind of saying

00:20:29.720 --> 00:20:34.330
which God and I think that's
a question worth addressing

00:20:34.330 --> 00:20:36.840
because the God that I believe in

00:20:36.840 --> 00:20:39.600
that is the God of the Bible is eternal

00:20:39.600 --> 00:20:42.827
and that raises problems
for the deadness doesn't it?

00:20:42.827 --> 00:20:43.739
- [Justin] Sure.

00:20:43.739 --> 00:20:45.200
- But there's a sense

00:20:45.200 --> 00:20:48.840
in which Nietzsche was
a very accurate prophet

00:20:48.840 --> 00:20:51.950
and what to my mind is very important

00:20:51.950 --> 00:20:54.940
with him was that he could see

00:20:54.940 --> 00:20:58.100
where many contemporary
atheists cannot see

00:20:58.100 --> 00:21:01.540
that if you abolish God,

00:21:01.540 --> 00:21:05.630
you wake the ground under any solidity

00:21:05.630 --> 00:21:08.900
on which you can base on
morality, human dignity,

00:21:08.900 --> 00:21:10.840
freedom, all those values.

00:21:10.840 --> 00:21:13.310
He saw that connection and he said,

00:21:13.310 --> 00:21:16.793
if you didn't bring God you've
no right in the end to value.

00:21:16.793 --> 00:21:19.457
So that you notice where
the values are real

00:21:19.457 --> 00:21:22.490
out in our society they're
mostly values that go back

00:21:22.490 --> 00:21:25.137
to the Judeo-Christian tradition.

00:21:25.137 --> 00:21:26.440
And I think, therefore,

00:21:26.440 --> 00:21:29.960
to bring that back into the discussion.

00:21:29.960 --> 00:21:34.270
I like the idea and I think
it's very important start

00:21:34.270 --> 00:21:36.770
where there's something more,

00:21:36.770 --> 00:21:39.450
there's something outside of us.

00:21:39.450 --> 00:21:41.410
That's the start, it seems to me,

00:21:41.410 --> 00:21:44.247
of coming back to something

00:21:44.247 --> 00:21:46.770
around which society can be organized

00:21:46.770 --> 00:21:50.070
because otherwise
everything is subjective.

00:21:50.070 --> 00:21:52.910
And you mentioned postmodern.

00:21:52.910 --> 00:21:56.770
And it amuses me that
so many, but it's sad,

00:21:56.770 --> 00:21:59.210
that so many of these people will tell you

00:21:59.210 --> 00:22:02.382
as an absolute truth
that there is no truth.

00:22:02.382 --> 00:22:03.627
Now that's just sheer nonsense.

00:22:03.627 --> 00:22:04.460
(audience laughing)

00:22:04.460 --> 00:22:06.819
- [Justin] Yeah because it's
a contradiction in terms.

00:22:06.819 --> 00:22:07.652
- Yes.

00:22:07.652 --> 00:22:08.730
- I mean you you obviously spend

00:22:08.730 --> 00:22:10.850
quite a lot of time
speaking and especially

00:22:10.850 --> 00:22:12.490
to actually a lot of young people.

00:22:12.490 --> 00:22:13.323
- Yes, I do.

00:22:13.323 --> 00:22:14.940
- Many of the events you do, John.

00:22:14.940 --> 00:22:18.470
Do you find that there's a kind
of people are looking again

00:22:18.470 --> 00:22:21.470
for a source of meaning,
for something to hold onto?

00:22:21.470 --> 00:22:26.050
- Absolutely, I think that, well we,

00:22:26.050 --> 00:22:29.170
it's country-specific because
there are parts of the world,

00:22:29.170 --> 00:22:31.670
of course and where, for
example Christianity,

00:22:31.670 --> 00:22:33.480
is growing like wildfire.

00:22:33.480 --> 00:22:35.570
But in the UK and in the U.S.

00:22:35.570 --> 00:22:40.260
I find that young people find the world

00:22:40.260 --> 00:22:44.240
that is presented to them by people

00:22:44.240 --> 00:22:48.120
like the old New Atheists or
the naturalistic philosophy

00:22:48.120 --> 00:22:49.720
is too small to live in.

00:22:49.720 --> 00:22:53.090
It doesn't give them any
kind of solid foundation.

00:22:53.090 --> 00:22:54.600
So they're looking again.

00:22:54.600 --> 00:22:56.490
And just what, last week,

00:22:56.490 --> 00:22:59.680
I spoke to Central Hall Westminster,

00:22:59.680 --> 00:23:04.443
1,500 young people starting
at the age of 13 to 18.

00:23:05.360 --> 00:23:08.340
Absolutely fascinating
spending a whole day thinking

00:23:08.340 --> 00:23:09.623
about these big ideas.

00:23:09.623 --> 00:23:12.030
That I find enormously encouraging

00:23:12.030 --> 00:23:15.080
when young folks start
asking these questions.

00:23:15.080 --> 00:23:18.420
And I find a huge
response around the world.

00:23:18.420 --> 00:23:20.127
But it's self selecting, you know,

00:23:20.127 --> 00:23:21.880
it's very difficult for me

00:23:21.880 --> 00:23:24.170
to give a global and fair assessment.

00:23:24.170 --> 00:23:25.003
- I mean, it strikes me, Dave,

00:23:25.003 --> 00:23:26.600
that you did this 100 city tour

00:23:26.600 --> 00:23:27.983
with Jordan Peterson and it strikes me

00:23:27.983 --> 00:23:31.900
that the crowds that have
flocked to hear him talk

00:23:31.900 --> 00:23:36.313
about meaning in a meaningful
way have been quite young.

00:23:37.470 --> 00:23:40.220
The kind of crowd that you kind
of might have expected more

00:23:40.220 --> 00:23:42.860
to be turning out to hear the Sam Harris's

00:23:42.860 --> 00:23:46.992
and the Christopher Hitchens
and Richard Dawkins and so on.

00:23:46.992 --> 00:23:48.590
What's changed?

00:23:48.590 --> 00:23:51.070
Why, in a sense, is the
conversation moved on

00:23:51.070 --> 00:23:52.932
from well we all know God's dead to.

00:23:52.932 --> 00:23:55.670
well what's gonna happen next, I suppose?

00:23:55.670 --> 00:23:56.540
- Well it's really interesting

00:23:56.540 --> 00:23:58.170
because if you were paying attention

00:23:58.170 --> 00:24:01.490
to the media around Jordan during the year

00:24:01.490 --> 00:24:04.650
the implication somehow or
the condemnation I should say,

00:24:04.650 --> 00:24:06.420
from the media was that somehow this was

00:24:06.420 --> 00:24:09.870
for young straight white men.

00:24:09.870 --> 00:24:12.060
That he's only talking
to straight white men

00:24:12.060 --> 00:24:14.560
as if inherently that's
somehow an evil thing, right?

00:24:14.560 --> 00:24:17.300
So that was the idea that
somehow he's really broken

00:24:17.300 --> 00:24:20.100
that straight white men are
showing up to his events

00:24:20.100 --> 00:24:21.630
and that's somehow inherently evil.

00:24:21.630 --> 00:24:23.730
Now of course that's absurd if, let's say,

00:24:23.730 --> 00:24:26.130
he was talking to all
straight young white men,

00:24:26.130 --> 00:24:29.070
if he happened to have been
giving them something positive

00:24:29.070 --> 00:24:30.900
that could put a little
order in their life

00:24:30.900 --> 00:24:32.840
away from the chaos as he would put it

00:24:32.840 --> 00:24:34.470
that would actually be a wonderful thing.

00:24:34.470 --> 00:24:37.510
Of course the irony is that
actually wasn't even true at all

00:24:37.510 --> 00:24:39.320
because the crowds were
wonderfully diverse

00:24:39.320 --> 00:24:42.200
and I usually thought they
were about 60/40 male to female

00:24:42.200 --> 00:24:44.500
and age range all over
the place and all that.

00:24:45.890 --> 00:24:47.590
But to answer your question specifically,

00:24:47.590 --> 00:24:51.790
I think that we got,

00:24:51.790 --> 00:24:53.830
as politics and the media

00:24:53.830 --> 00:24:56.970
and social media and the fact
that we're all walking around

00:24:56.970 --> 00:24:59.890
with a phone in our pocket
that has the world's knowledge

00:24:59.890 --> 00:25:03.710
and you can connect with somebody
literally across the world

00:25:03.710 --> 00:25:06.970
in a split second, I think we have no idea

00:25:06.970 --> 00:25:11.970
how this information orgy basically,

00:25:12.330 --> 00:25:15.190
I think we have no idea how
it's affecting our brains,

00:25:15.190 --> 00:25:17.220
our ability to think clearly about things.

00:25:17.220 --> 00:25:18.810
It's doing wondrous things, right?

00:25:18.810 --> 00:25:21.890
We're all here because of
this, this a podcast, right?

00:25:21.890 --> 00:25:24.880
We're disseminating this
through the digital world,

00:25:24.880 --> 00:25:28.570
this is incredible but it
also has destabilized sort

00:25:28.570 --> 00:25:32.180
of basic beliefs and I
think then Jordan stepped in

00:25:32.180 --> 00:25:35.920
and said we have to be able to
get some meaning out of this.

00:25:35.920 --> 00:25:38.160
So that's why he wrote "12 Rules."

00:25:38.160 --> 00:25:40.340
He thought these are 12
rules in a modern sense.

00:25:40.340 --> 00:25:42.930
He wasn't handing down
the Ten Commandments again

00:25:42.930 --> 00:25:44.800
but he was saying in a modern sense

00:25:44.800 --> 00:25:46.690
these are 12 things you can do.

00:25:46.690 --> 00:25:48.440
Stand up straight with your shoulder back,

00:25:48.440 --> 00:25:50.510
with your shoulders back, clean your room,

00:25:50.510 --> 00:25:53.080
clean your room before
you clean the world.

00:25:53.080 --> 00:25:55.590
These are basic things
where now we have people

00:25:55.590 --> 00:25:57.570
that want to fix the world constantly

00:25:57.570 --> 00:25:59.030
that can't fix themselves.

00:25:59.030 --> 00:26:00.383
They're doing it backwards.

00:26:01.260 --> 00:26:04.561
But just very briefly on the underpinning

00:26:04.561 --> 00:26:08.800
of some sort of belief
that can lead to freedom

00:26:08.800 --> 00:26:09.653
and that's what sort of what

00:26:09.653 --> 00:26:11.930
I was talking about
about the Enlightenment.

00:26:11.930 --> 00:26:12.940
Think about, you know,

00:26:12.940 --> 00:26:14.690
the founding documents of this country

00:26:14.690 --> 00:26:17.300
I think are the greatest
man written documents,

00:26:17.300 --> 00:26:19.360
let's say, political documents at least.

00:26:19.360 --> 00:26:20.770
And what did the Founders say?

00:26:20.770 --> 00:26:24.690
They said these are God-given
rights, they're self-evident.

00:26:24.690 --> 00:26:26.910
We did not give you these rights,

00:26:26.910 --> 00:26:29.020
the right of freedom and
of free speech all things.

00:26:29.020 --> 00:26:31.740
We can protect these things
but we didn't give them to you

00:26:31.740 --> 00:26:33.770
because they came from somewhere else.

00:26:33.770 --> 00:26:35.800
That is so deeply important

00:26:35.800 --> 00:26:38.730
and in many ways very unique to America

00:26:38.730 --> 00:26:40.880
and that's why there's
such a bizarre assault

00:26:40.880 --> 00:26:42.300
on freedom of speech right now

00:26:42.300 --> 00:26:45.330
and actually almost everything
in the Bill of Rights

00:26:45.330 --> 00:26:48.960
and it comes mostly
from the secular world.

00:26:48.960 --> 00:26:51.360
That is a really sad twist

00:26:51.360 --> 00:26:54.360
that truly I would not have
expected a couple years ago

00:26:54.360 --> 00:26:55.970
even as someone that saw this coming.

00:26:55.970 --> 00:26:59.610
I mean five years ago I
was waving me the flag,

00:26:59.610 --> 00:27:01.810
going guys there's something
happening here on the Left,

00:27:01.810 --> 00:27:04.250
this progressive thing, this no good.

00:27:04.250 --> 00:27:06.460
But even, it's gotten so crazy,

00:27:06.460 --> 00:27:09.100
that I'm still a little surprised myself.

00:27:09.100 --> 00:27:11.143
- To some extent it's almost as though

00:27:11.143 --> 00:27:14.550
that the meaning crisis has
almost created this vacuum

00:27:14.550 --> 00:27:18.260
and people are finding all
kinds of crazy things to do.

00:27:18.260 --> 00:27:20.320
- Oh yeah, they're looking
anywhere you can look,

00:27:20.320 --> 00:27:21.906
you can play video games all day,

00:27:21.906 --> 00:27:26.906
you can do whatever it
is to fill up that hole

00:27:26.980 --> 00:27:28.680
if it's an existential hole

00:27:28.680 --> 00:27:30.870
or a hole in belief or whatever it is.

00:27:30.870 --> 00:27:33.000
But there's a lot of
ways to fill that hole.

00:27:33.000 --> 00:27:38.000
I think Jordan, Jordan in my
opinion has given the best set

00:27:38.160 --> 00:27:41.660
of beliefs that take from
a religious tradition

00:27:41.660 --> 00:27:44.390
and blend what I would say
our enlightenment values

00:27:44.390 --> 00:27:47.130
or basically secular values,
Judeo-Christian values

00:27:47.130 --> 00:27:51.140
and he's blended them in
the most effective way.

00:27:51.140 --> 00:27:52.560
- I mean, as we're on the subject,

00:27:52.560 --> 00:27:53.780
talking about Jordan Peterson,

00:27:53.780 --> 00:27:55.500
I know that you're somewhat familiar

00:27:55.500 --> 00:27:57.380
with some of what he's
been doing as well, John.

00:27:57.380 --> 00:28:00.623
What's your take on what
he's put his finger on,

00:28:00.623 --> 00:28:03.880
that obviously so many
people are responding to,

00:28:03.880 --> 00:28:06.640
and, yeah, well how does
it relate in your view

00:28:06.640 --> 00:28:09.060
to the Christian faith that you hold.

00:28:09.060 --> 00:28:12.850
- I think people are longing for sense.

00:28:12.850 --> 00:28:16.230
And you mentioned connectedness.

00:28:16.230 --> 00:28:18.440
That has almost replaced meaning

00:28:18.440 --> 00:28:21.550
but it's not real connectedness.

00:28:21.550 --> 00:28:24.110
And I was reading a book

00:28:24.110 --> 00:28:27.220
on artificial intelligence just recently

00:28:27.220 --> 00:28:31.180
and it was a warning

00:28:31.180 --> 00:28:34.860
that people will die

00:28:34.860 --> 00:28:36.850
if they're not connected to the internet

00:28:36.850 --> 00:28:39.430
because all the meaning
is being placed there.

00:28:39.430 --> 00:28:41.730
I mean, think what
Jordan Peterson is doing

00:28:41.730 --> 00:28:44.780
is putting a nuclear bomb
in the middle of that

00:28:44.780 --> 00:28:47.140
and saying this not good enough.

00:28:47.140 --> 00:28:50.710
You've got to get outside of that.

00:28:50.710 --> 00:28:54.040
And you're right it is rewiring brains.

00:28:54.040 --> 00:28:57.600
The psychologists tell us it's
messing people's brains up,

00:28:57.600 --> 00:29:01.030
especially if they try and
use two machines at once.

00:29:01.030 --> 00:29:06.030
And therefore I just think
that there's an underlying,

00:29:06.650 --> 00:29:10.870
and from where I sit,
people are looking for this

00:29:10.870 --> 00:29:13.530
because although they
often don't believe it

00:29:13.530 --> 00:29:15.470
or even have never heard of it,

00:29:15.470 --> 00:29:17.360
they are made in the image of God.

00:29:17.360 --> 00:29:21.780
They're beings who've got
eternity in their hearts.

00:29:21.780 --> 00:29:24.740
And a kind of materialist universe

00:29:24.740 --> 00:29:26.990
without meaning just won't satisfy them

00:29:26.990 --> 00:29:30.190
because they're actually
made for something bigger.

00:29:30.190 --> 00:29:32.757
And CS Lewis put it years ago,

00:29:32.757 --> 00:29:37.757
"If you find a lie in you that's
not satisfied in this world

00:29:38.397 --> 00:29:40.547
"maybe there is another world

00:29:40.547 --> 00:29:42.380
"in which it could be satisfied."

00:29:42.380 --> 00:29:45.270
And I'm gonna play that
to the world of ideas.

00:29:45.270 --> 00:29:47.890
So I really think he's hit a nerve.

00:29:47.890 --> 00:29:50.477
- And interestingly if you read his book,

00:29:50.477 --> 00:29:51.440
"12 Rules For Life,"

00:29:51.440 --> 00:29:52.853
and he's done a number

00:29:52.853 --> 00:29:54.062
of lectures.
- Yes, I have.

00:29:54.062 --> 00:29:55.950
- Is he's drawing a
great deal on the Bible

00:29:55.950 --> 00:29:56.870
especially the Old Testament.

00:29:56.870 --> 00:30:00.280
- Oh, sure, he is,
especially the Old Testament.

00:30:00.280 --> 00:30:01.550
And it has intrigued me

00:30:01.550 --> 00:30:05.350
that he has concentrated his lectures

00:30:05.350 --> 00:30:07.370
of the Bible on the Old Testament.

00:30:07.370 --> 00:30:10.420
Now that to me resonates completely

00:30:10.420 --> 00:30:15.420
because I, as a Christian,
have been for years trying

00:30:15.570 --> 00:30:18.800
to communicate to people
that they've got to begin

00:30:18.800 --> 00:30:21.840
to take the Jewish Scriptures seriously

00:30:21.840 --> 00:30:23.840
because there you've got the foundation,

00:30:24.850 --> 00:30:26.360
the foundation story

00:30:27.353 --> 00:30:30.500
and people are looking for
a story that's big enough

00:30:30.500 --> 00:30:33.530
to fit their lives into
and there's the start

00:30:33.530 --> 00:30:35.963
of the big story, creation,

00:30:36.970 --> 00:30:40.570
human beings made in the image of God

00:30:40.570 --> 00:30:42.900
and what that means for their dignity

00:30:42.900 --> 00:30:45.260
and their freedom and everything else.

00:30:45.260 --> 00:30:47.560
If we just get that one fact.

00:30:47.560 --> 00:30:51.800
I remember in Siberia where
I used to go quite a bit

00:30:51.800 --> 00:30:55.590
and I gave the first lecture
in 75 years on these issues

00:30:55.590 --> 00:30:57.240
in the University of Novosibirsk.

00:30:58.694 --> 00:31:01.367
And I made the point, I said,

00:31:01.367 --> 00:31:04.053
"Just think of the one statement.

00:31:04.927 --> 00:31:07.290
"Human beings made in the image of God."

00:31:08.800 --> 00:31:13.677
And I said, "If I believed
that they wouldn't murder one

00:31:13.677 --> 00:31:16.510
"of you let alone the 100
million that Stalin did."

00:31:16.510 --> 00:31:19.000
And it absolutely erupted the place.

00:31:19.000 --> 00:31:20.940
Of course they'd never heard it before.

00:31:20.940 --> 00:31:22.697
It was totally new to them.

00:31:22.697 --> 00:31:26.930
And I think our society
needs to hear these truths.

00:31:26.930 --> 00:31:31.910
And Jordan Peterson is
moving into that area

00:31:31.910 --> 00:31:35.240
because he's actually
going back to the book

00:31:35.240 --> 00:31:37.400
and not being ashamed of it.

00:31:37.400 --> 00:31:39.860
And more power to him, I say.

00:31:39.860 --> 00:31:41.110
- Yeah, and just very briefly,

00:31:41.110 --> 00:31:43.920
just from being on tour with Jordan,

00:31:43.920 --> 00:31:44.980
I told you this backstage

00:31:44.980 --> 00:31:49.570
but I never saw the guy
break one of the 12 rules.

00:31:49.570 --> 00:31:52.290
I mean try to imagine
how chaotic his life was

00:31:52.290 --> 00:31:54.250
in the course of this,
becoming a massive star,

00:31:54.250 --> 00:31:56.920
traveling all over the
world, the book sales,

00:31:56.920 --> 00:31:58.930
the celebrity, the entire thing,

00:31:58.930 --> 00:32:02.300
and he never broke one of those 12 rules.

00:32:02.300 --> 00:32:05.200
Just 20 seconds we were at a dinner party

00:32:05.200 --> 00:32:07.890
at Douglas Murray's house
with Maajid Nawaz and Jordan,

00:32:07.890 --> 00:32:09.010
you may know those guys.

00:32:09.010 --> 00:32:11.200
And one of the rules is
that if you see a pet,

00:32:11.200 --> 00:32:13.280
if you see a cat in the
street you should pet it.

00:32:13.280 --> 00:32:16.500
And Douglas had a cat and we're there

00:32:16.500 --> 00:32:18.410
for about three or four
hours, we're having dinner,

00:32:18.410 --> 00:32:19.280
we're having a great time

00:32:19.280 --> 00:32:20.580
and I'm looking at the cat the whole time

00:32:20.580 --> 00:32:21.413
and I'm looking at Jordan.

00:32:21.413 --> 00:32:24.710
And I'm going the guy hasn't
pet the cat, like you know,

00:32:24.710 --> 00:32:26.687
what am I on tour with this guy for?

00:32:26.687 --> 00:32:28.320
Like, is the whole thing
is it, is he a fraud,

00:32:28.320 --> 00:32:29.750
what's going on here?

00:32:29.750 --> 00:32:31.750
I swear to God I was
thinking it the entire time

00:32:31.750 --> 00:32:34.460
and then as we were walking out the door,

00:32:34.460 --> 00:32:36.790
Jordan literally and you can
sort of picture Jordan's,

00:32:36.790 --> 00:32:40.400
he's very tall and he has
long limbs and he's slender

00:32:40.400 --> 00:32:45.030
and he basically sat down in
the cat's bed with the cat

00:32:45.030 --> 00:32:47.950
and stroked the cat for
a good five minutes.

00:32:47.950 --> 00:32:49.948
And I thought all right
he's the real deal.

00:32:49.948 --> 00:32:52.130
(audience laughing)

00:32:52.130 --> 00:32:53.330
- We should probably talk about someone

00:32:53.330 --> 00:32:55.597
other than Jordan Peterson tonight.

00:32:55.597 --> 00:33:00.597
I mean I'd love to key in a
little bit more on your story

00:33:01.230 --> 00:33:02.063
Dave--
- Sure.

00:33:02.063 --> 00:33:03.730
- Because it sounds like you have been

00:33:03.730 --> 00:33:06.780
on something of a journey of discovery

00:33:06.780 --> 00:33:08.010
over the last year or two

00:33:08.010 --> 00:33:09.430
and obviously very much influenced

00:33:09.430 --> 00:33:11.370
by the way Jordan has brought that alive

00:33:11.370 --> 00:33:12.970
in his lectures and so on.

00:33:12.970 --> 00:33:16.610
So I mean you obviously now
are seeing more and more

00:33:16.610 --> 00:33:19.840
the value of religion.

00:33:19.840 --> 00:33:21.700
What does that caused you personally to do

00:33:21.700 --> 00:33:24.070
in terms of maybe, is it causing you

00:33:24.070 --> 00:33:26.160
to rediscover your own
Jewish roots a bit more

00:33:26.160 --> 00:33:27.893
and the religious aspect of that?

00:33:29.560 --> 00:33:33.010
- Well, I would say
there's two things here

00:33:33.010 --> 00:33:34.290
as I said at the beginning.

00:33:34.290 --> 00:33:36.200
There's sort of just the cultural affinity

00:33:36.200 --> 00:33:39.960
and the understanding of
the history of the people

00:33:39.960 --> 00:33:42.280
that came before me which
you know unfortunately

00:33:42.280 --> 00:33:44.630
in the case of the Jews is a pretty brutal

00:33:45.490 --> 00:33:48.713
often almost unimaginably
horrific history.

00:33:50.150 --> 00:33:54.620
And I grew up around Holocaust
survivors and I know that.

00:33:54.620 --> 00:33:56.910
That, though the pain of your ancestors

00:33:56.910 --> 00:33:59.650
or whatever the history of your
people is can't be the thing

00:33:59.650 --> 00:34:02.240
that defines you going forward.

00:34:02.240 --> 00:34:04.180
I would say as I've sat down

00:34:04.180 --> 00:34:07.350
with believers and non-believers alike,

00:34:07.350 --> 00:34:09.513
I've genuinely found,

00:34:10.770 --> 00:34:11.950
well I guess sort of this would get

00:34:11.950 --> 00:34:13.190
to what you were saying, John.

00:34:13.190 --> 00:34:15.170
I have generally found the believers

00:34:16.050 --> 00:34:19.170
not only more welcoming so
like in a situation like this

00:34:20.070 --> 00:34:24.930
but more open, actually
happier, less dependent on

00:34:26.320 --> 00:34:29.723
things outside of themselves,
more self-reliant, let's say.

00:34:30.620 --> 00:34:33.390
So I don't think that means

00:34:33.390 --> 00:34:36.550
I'm going to be religious per se tomorrow.

00:34:36.550 --> 00:34:38.480
That being said, as I said,

00:34:38.480 --> 00:34:42.130
I went to this Yom Kippur
service that Dennis Prager hosted

00:34:42.130 --> 00:34:44.650
and I found it incredibly moving.

00:34:44.650 --> 00:34:46.830
And Dennis gave a sermon that, you know,

00:34:46.830 --> 00:34:48.730
he talked all about Judeo-Christian values

00:34:48.730 --> 00:34:51.860
and sort of what's happening
in our country right now

00:34:51.860 --> 00:34:54.690
and how it all seems to
be becoming untethered

00:34:54.690 --> 00:34:59.010
and he used some religious
backing to give some value there.

00:34:59.010 --> 00:35:00.530
And I thought well this is value,

00:35:00.530 --> 00:35:03.100
this something in a real world way

00:35:03.100 --> 00:35:05.540
that I could come somewhere once a week

00:35:05.540 --> 00:35:08.240
or build some community around or friends

00:35:08.240 --> 00:35:09.410
that would have value.

00:35:09.410 --> 00:35:14.410
So I can't say I'm, you
know it's like if ultimately

00:35:14.510 --> 00:35:17.030
I can see in your eye
you want me to be like,

00:35:17.030 --> 00:35:18.742
yeah, yeah, Jesus let it go.

00:35:18.742 --> 00:35:21.600
(audience laughing)

00:35:21.600 --> 00:35:22.433
I can see it.

00:35:22.433 --> 00:35:25.480
There's a guy back there
waving a Jesus sign at me.

00:35:25.480 --> 00:35:26.533
All right, I see you.

00:35:27.542 --> 00:35:29.454
(audience laughing)

00:35:29.454 --> 00:35:30.287
- [Justin] Well, if you insist.

00:35:30.287 --> 00:35:34.333
- Yeah, let's put it this way.

00:35:34.333 --> 00:35:37.210
I have no problem with
Jesus, I like the guy.

00:35:37.210 --> 00:35:38.043
You know what I mean?

00:35:38.043 --> 00:35:40.090
Like the message of Jesus
that all of the things

00:35:40.090 --> 00:35:42.270
that we've talked about
here on stage and backstage,

00:35:42.270 --> 00:35:43.620
I love these ideas.

00:35:43.620 --> 00:35:48.050
I think that if my life becomes
a continuing conversation

00:35:48.050 --> 00:35:49.770
about these things

00:35:49.770 --> 00:35:51.900
and I can incorporate
the best parts of that

00:35:51.900 --> 00:35:53.210
to be a better person?

00:35:53.210 --> 00:35:54.043
Well, I'll tell you this.

00:35:54.043 --> 00:35:57.320
I know for a fact, I am
a better person today

00:35:57.320 --> 00:35:59.750
than I was before I started
this journey with Jordan.

00:35:59.750 --> 00:36:02.110
That those things, and that
doesn't mean I prescribe

00:36:02.110 --> 00:36:03.830
to the Church of Jordan Peterson,

00:36:03.830 --> 00:36:05.170
that is just that this guy

00:36:05.170 --> 00:36:09.000
who has communicated the bedrock ideas

00:36:09.000 --> 00:36:10.500
that we're talking about here,

00:36:11.420 --> 00:36:13.150
by me listening to that and hearing that

00:36:13.150 --> 00:36:14.500
and incorporating some of that in my life,

00:36:14.500 --> 00:36:15.430
I'm a better person.

00:36:15.430 --> 00:36:19.210
So that means something,
you know what I mean?

00:36:19.210 --> 00:36:21.783
- Any comments on Dave's journey so far?

00:36:22.630 --> 00:36:24.113
- Feel like "This is My life."

00:36:24.113 --> 00:36:24.946
(audience laughing)

00:36:24.946 --> 00:36:26.634
- You're on--
- all right.

00:36:26.634 --> 00:36:28.263
- [Justin] The
psychiatrists couch tonight.

00:36:29.210 --> 00:36:31.833
- Well I think what
struck me, just listening,

00:36:32.820 --> 00:36:36.073
was you're being moved by Yom Kippur.

00:36:37.800 --> 00:36:39.910
I'm not Jewish by background

00:36:41.120 --> 00:36:45.710
but I owe everything to a Jew

00:36:47.260 --> 00:36:51.010
and the history of the Jews has been

00:36:51.010 --> 00:36:53.870
enormously important to me.

00:36:53.870 --> 00:36:56.900
And when you mention
the Holocaust as you do

00:36:56.900 --> 00:36:58.877
and I've been in Auschwitz many times.

00:36:58.877 --> 00:37:00.883
And I've wept every time.

00:37:02.002 --> 00:37:04.520
And I have many Jewish
friends who lost everybody

00:37:04.520 --> 00:37:08.983
and that raises huge, deep,
existential questions.

00:37:09.930 --> 00:37:13.860
And therefore just thinking
of the big picture,

00:37:13.860 --> 00:37:18.860
the big biblical story and I love it

00:37:19.760 --> 00:37:21.560
because although there are all sorts

00:37:21.560 --> 00:37:24.870
of twists and turns and difficulties,

00:37:24.870 --> 00:37:29.870
I see the Jewish history,
the history of Israel,

00:37:30.950 --> 00:37:35.950
the law, the prophets as
pointing towards something big

00:37:37.480 --> 00:37:39.723
because at the heart of Judaism

00:37:39.723 --> 00:37:41.410
and I have many Orthodox friends

00:37:41.410 --> 00:37:45.950
who still expect Hamashiach,
the Messiah, to come.

00:37:45.950 --> 00:37:50.313
Now the difference is that
I believe he has come.

00:37:51.520 --> 00:37:54.360
And Yom Kippur means a huge amount to me

00:37:54.360 --> 00:37:57.680
because in those Jewish festivals,

00:37:57.680 --> 00:37:59.800
and I've been at many of them,

00:37:59.800 --> 00:38:03.310
I see, I don't want to put a crudely

00:38:03.310 --> 00:38:07.450
but a thought model
that has been fulfilled

00:38:07.450 --> 00:38:09.600
in what Jesus did.

00:38:09.600 --> 00:38:12.790
And I find Yom Kippur moving

00:38:12.790 --> 00:38:16.520
because I see in him the fulfillment of it

00:38:16.520 --> 00:38:21.520
and here is a person who actually
died the Day of Atonement.

00:38:22.550 --> 00:38:24.810
It's an atonement to deal with.

00:38:24.810 --> 00:38:26.650
Now here comes the point.

00:38:26.650 --> 00:38:29.570
That what happens when you start

00:38:29.570 --> 00:38:31.780
with a creation story of people made

00:38:31.780 --> 00:38:34.300
in the image of God, that's wonderful.

00:38:34.300 --> 00:38:36.300
But we know that something has happened,

00:38:36.300 --> 00:38:38.670
a bomb has hit the human race.

00:38:38.670 --> 00:38:43.670
There are huge problems
and we long for a solution.

00:38:44.210 --> 00:38:45.450
We long for justice.

00:38:45.450 --> 00:38:47.100
We long for true freedom.

00:38:47.100 --> 00:38:49.200
We long for true values.

00:38:49.200 --> 00:38:53.050
And we've got to
therefore face the problem

00:38:53.050 --> 00:38:55.057
of human rebellion against God.

00:38:55.057 --> 00:38:58.350
Now the sin word is
not popular these days.

00:38:58.350 --> 00:39:01.090
But it seems to me that
there's a fulfillment

00:39:02.580 --> 00:39:05.530
within what Jesus did and taught,

00:39:05.530 --> 00:39:09.200
a fulfillment that everything
that Judaism stood for

00:39:09.200 --> 00:39:11.860
and stands for powerfully.

00:39:11.860 --> 00:39:15.890
And therefore I feel
a close affinity here,

00:39:15.890 --> 00:39:20.890
the whole Judeo tradition
is immensely important to me

00:39:21.270 --> 00:39:23.670
because there I find
these fundamental values.

00:39:23.670 --> 00:39:26.980
But they raise a big question

00:39:26.980 --> 00:39:30.743
and it's the fulfillment of that,

00:39:31.770 --> 00:39:35.100
the whole history of
Israel, its sacrifices,

00:39:35.100 --> 00:39:39.470
the institutions, the prophets
looking forward to Messiah

00:39:39.470 --> 00:39:42.943
who will deal with the basic
problem of human rebellion.

00:39:43.988 --> 00:39:47.460
And so that's clearly a
difference between me and Judaism.

00:39:47.460 --> 00:39:52.460
But I wouldn't underemphasize
the huge contribution

00:39:52.730 --> 00:39:55.350
that it has made to the rock

00:39:55.350 --> 00:39:59.230
on which I believe I stand
today, if that makes sense.

00:39:59.230 --> 00:40:00.810
- Well it does make sense
and if I could quickly,

00:40:00.810 --> 00:40:02.100
I actually do want to answer

00:40:02.100 --> 00:40:03.680
