00:41:53.910 --> 00:41:56.750
but I want to start to investigate

00:41:56.750 --> 00:41:58.740
and live into this kind of tradition more

00:41:58.740 --> 00:42:01.123
that obviously your
forebears have done so.

00:42:02.630 --> 00:42:06.150
I mean there's a lot of people out there

00:42:06.150 --> 00:42:10.407
who would say, "Come on, wake
up, it's the 21st century.

00:42:10.407 --> 00:42:13.807
"We all know that these
are just superstitions

00:42:13.807 --> 00:42:15.697
"and everything else and you need to get

00:42:15.697 --> 00:42:17.680
"on the bandwagon of reason."

00:42:17.680 --> 00:42:19.420
You're about reason, Dave.

00:42:19.420 --> 00:42:22.260
And if we just think straight,

00:42:22.260 --> 00:42:24.640
work things out with science and logic

00:42:24.640 --> 00:42:27.570
then that's the way
forward, that's the way you,

00:42:27.570 --> 00:42:29.400
religion is kind of the way

00:42:29.400 --> 00:42:31.040
we used to do things not anymore.

00:42:31.040 --> 00:42:32.970
- Yeah, well I think the
counter to that would be

00:42:32.970 --> 00:42:34.870
that everything that
Jordan has talked about,

00:42:34.870 --> 00:42:36.080
not to bring this back to Jordan,

00:42:36.080 --> 00:42:39.850
but the counter of that
is that we'll look sort of

00:42:39.850 --> 00:42:43.670
where the secular world is
at where we can't figure out

00:42:43.670 --> 00:42:45.270
whether you're male or female anymore

00:42:45.270 --> 00:42:46.500
we have to now debate that.

00:42:46.500 --> 00:42:48.730
I don't it's sort of like
I don't like using that one

00:42:48.730 --> 00:42:51.260
because it's so easy and
it sounds sort of glib

00:42:51.260 --> 00:42:52.830
and I don't mean it to be that way.

00:42:52.830 --> 00:42:55.340
But that is where this all leading.

00:42:55.340 --> 00:42:58.000
If there is nothing outside
of ourselves then, John,

00:42:58.000 --> 00:43:00.050
as you said everything else is subjective

00:43:00.050 --> 00:43:02.590
and we will debate every little thing

00:43:02.590 --> 00:43:06.450
depending on how we feel
about it on any given day.

00:43:06.450 --> 00:43:09.470
And that will lead,
there is there's a reason

00:43:09.470 --> 00:43:13.010
why right now the idea of
socialism is suddenly popping up

00:43:13.010 --> 00:43:16.240
in America which is the
genuinely the worst set

00:43:16.240 --> 00:43:18.730
of collectivist ideas that
you could possibly ever have

00:43:18.730 --> 00:43:19.750
that hundreds of millions

00:43:19.750 --> 00:43:22.410
of people have died in history under.

00:43:22.410 --> 00:43:24.750
And it's popping up because if you listen

00:43:24.750 --> 00:43:28.840
to what's happening on the
Left right now politically

00:43:28.840 --> 00:43:30.450
because they've outsourced God,

00:43:30.450 --> 00:43:32.960
imagine if one of those
people on stage said

00:43:32.960 --> 00:43:34.380
that they were a real believer.

00:43:34.380 --> 00:43:37.150
Imagine if any of them,
maybe Biden could do it,

00:43:37.150 --> 00:43:39.190
but really the rest of them can't.

00:43:39.190 --> 00:43:41.020
They would never really say
that they're a believer.

00:43:41.020 --> 00:43:41.853
Now I don't know what they are

00:43:41.853 --> 00:43:43.150
and I wouldn't want them to say anything

00:43:43.150 --> 00:43:45.730
that's not true to themselves

00:43:45.730 --> 00:43:48.840
But they would be mocked
by everything mainstream,

00:43:48.840 --> 00:43:51.260
everything mainstream
would mock them the way

00:43:51.260 --> 00:43:54.770
that everything mainstream
mocks any Christian

00:43:54.770 --> 00:43:56.230
that happens, 'cause they're
usually on the Right,

00:43:56.230 --> 00:43:58.530
they happen to be conservatives.

00:43:58.530 --> 00:44:00.870
But look what they're offering us now.

00:44:00.870 --> 00:44:03.530
Their answer is, okay we've
removed God from the equation

00:44:03.530 --> 00:44:05.250
and what do we get, we get government.

00:44:05.250 --> 00:44:07.790
And they now pray basically to government.

00:44:07.790 --> 00:44:10.930
They think that they
can figure out somehow

00:44:10.930 --> 00:44:13.290
by sitting in a room with a
bunch of other politicians

00:44:13.290 --> 00:44:16.622
and bureaucrats, the worst
sort of people that exist,

00:44:16.622 --> 00:44:19.070
(audience laughing)

00:44:19.070 --> 00:44:21.520
I didn't even mean that
to be funny but actually.

00:44:22.694 --> 00:44:23.850
I mean, but that's what they think.

00:44:23.850 --> 00:44:27.920
They think that they can
rejigger all of humanity

00:44:27.920 --> 00:44:30.200
in a way that will be so much better

00:44:30.200 --> 00:44:32.500
than everything that came before them.

00:44:32.500 --> 00:44:33.880
And not only can't they,

00:44:33.880 --> 00:44:37.420
they are going to do the complete reverse.

00:44:37.420 --> 00:44:40.710
So that, if for no other reason,

00:44:40.710 --> 00:44:44.160
if for no grand revelation
or something like that,

00:44:44.160 --> 00:44:46.160
that would be a reason to be respectful

00:44:46.160 --> 00:44:48.010
of people that are believers

00:44:48.010 --> 00:44:50.570
because they can fight that in
a way that secularists can't.

00:44:50.570 --> 00:44:53.230
The good liberals don't have enough juice

00:44:53.230 --> 00:44:54.970
in and of themselves,

00:44:54.970 --> 00:44:56.780
they don't have enough
juice to fight that.

00:44:56.780 --> 00:44:58.710
That's why liberalism has collapsed

00:44:58.710 --> 00:45:00.160
in the name of progressivism.

00:45:01.030 --> 00:45:04.060
- Yeah, I approach this in two ways.

00:45:04.060 --> 00:45:09.060
The first one is your comment
that secularism is collapsing.

00:45:09.240 --> 00:45:12.630
And one can analyze the defects of atheism

00:45:12.630 --> 00:45:14.540
where it leads to and
the millions of people

00:45:14.540 --> 00:45:16.380
that died in the last century.

00:45:16.380 --> 00:45:18.653
But coming over to the other side.

00:45:19.680 --> 00:45:24.490
What you're saying, Justin,
you see I am a scientist

00:45:24.490 --> 00:45:28.470
and one of the fascinating things is

00:45:28.470 --> 00:45:31.460
that science is a direct legacy

00:45:31.460 --> 00:45:33.810
of the Judeo-Christian tradition.

00:45:33.810 --> 00:45:37.110
You were saying we're all
scientists now and all this is,

00:45:37.110 --> 00:45:38.630
no, it is not.

00:45:38.630 --> 00:45:41.430
And let's start absolutely basic.

00:45:41.430 --> 00:45:44.240
In the beginning God created
the heavens and the earth.

00:45:44.240 --> 00:45:47.640
Hebrew Bible written millennia ago

00:45:47.640 --> 00:45:49.580
knew there was a beginning.

00:45:49.580 --> 00:45:51.030
It was in the early '60s

00:45:51.030 --> 00:45:53.193
before scientists caught up with that.

00:45:53.193 --> 00:45:55.195
(audience laughing)

00:45:55.195 --> 00:45:56.300
And the Bible was right.

00:45:56.300 --> 00:45:59.070
And Dawkins said, "Well
there was a 50/50 chance,"

00:45:59.070 --> 00:46:00.980
when I debated him.

00:46:00.980 --> 00:46:04.470
And I said, "At least
the Bible got it right."

00:46:04.470 --> 00:46:08.073
But more seriously than that, you see,

00:46:09.150 --> 00:46:12.763
the fact that I'm a mathematician
and interested in science,

00:46:13.738 --> 00:46:15.570
all right, just think about that.

00:46:15.570 --> 00:46:19.070
The fact that mathematics
can describe what goes on

00:46:19.070 --> 00:46:22.800
in the universe is a
matter of huge wonder.

00:46:22.800 --> 00:46:26.697
Einstein once said, "The
only incomprehensible thing

00:46:26.697 --> 00:46:29.520
"about the universe is
that it's comprehensible."

00:46:29.520 --> 00:46:31.600
And he saw the problem.

00:46:31.600 --> 00:46:33.170
Why does it work?

00:46:33.170 --> 00:46:36.510
Well it's not an incomprehensible thing

00:46:36.510 --> 00:46:40.670
if you start from the idea
that there's an intelligent God

00:46:40.670 --> 00:46:45.333
who made us in His image and
therefore we can do science.

00:46:45.333 --> 00:46:49.490
And that's exactly what the early pioneers

00:46:49.490 --> 00:46:51.780
of modern science, starting with Galileo

00:46:52.749 --> 00:46:55.890
and Kepler and Newton
and so on and so forth.

00:46:55.890 --> 00:46:58.250
They were all believers in God.

00:46:58.250 --> 00:47:02.240
And therefore when I hear
that kind of question

00:47:02.240 --> 00:47:05.190
I'm not remotely ashamed
of being a scientist

00:47:05.190 --> 00:47:07.450
and a Christian because I want to argue

00:47:07.450 --> 00:47:10.800
that it was Christianity
gave me my subject.

00:47:10.800 --> 00:47:12.563
And CS Lewis put it brilliantly.

00:47:14.105 --> 00:47:19.105
(audience applauding)
(audience laughing)

00:47:21.310 --> 00:47:23.850
I'm glad you understood it in the end.

00:47:23.850 --> 00:47:27.420
(audience laughing)

00:47:27.420 --> 00:47:30.407
CS Lewis said, "Men became scientific

00:47:30.407 --> 00:47:32.367
"because they expected law in nature.

00:47:32.367 --> 00:47:33.917
"And they expected law nature

00:47:33.917 --> 00:47:36.380
"because they believed in a lawgiver."

00:47:36.380 --> 00:47:38.670
And I think we're getting to the stage now

00:47:38.670 --> 00:47:43.330
where serious atheist
thinkers are beginning

00:47:43.330 --> 00:47:47.690
to reexamine the kind of naturalism

00:47:47.690 --> 00:47:50.240
that reduces everything
to physics and chemistry.

00:47:50.240 --> 00:47:53.730
And one of them lives in New
York, his name is Thomas Nagel

00:47:53.730 --> 00:47:55.620
and he's a brilliant philosopher.

00:47:55.620 --> 00:47:57.540
And he says something's going wrong

00:47:57.540 --> 00:48:00.740
because if everything is
reducible to physics and chemistry

00:48:00.740 --> 00:48:05.740
then so is your mind but then
why would you trust your mind?

00:48:06.340 --> 00:48:10.620
In other words atheism taken
to its logical conclusion

00:48:10.620 --> 00:48:13.060
undermines the very rationality you need

00:48:13.060 --> 00:48:15.160
to trust to do science.

00:48:15.160 --> 00:48:17.890
And I'm not in for accepting a worldview

00:48:17.890 --> 00:48:22.020
that undermines the foundations
of any kind of argument

00:48:22.020 --> 00:48:23.630
or discussion whatsoever.

00:48:23.630 --> 00:48:27.620
So I think that in the 21st century

00:48:27.620 --> 00:48:32.120
we can push back on that very naive notion

00:48:32.120 --> 00:48:36.950
that God's out, we do science now.

00:48:36.950 --> 00:48:41.573
No, science actually brings God back in.

00:48:42.620 --> 00:48:44.576
- It's very interesting, I mean.

00:48:44.576 --> 00:48:47.743
(audience applauding)

00:48:49.445 --> 00:48:53.700
All of this leads me to want
to ask you at this point, Dave,

00:48:53.700 --> 00:48:55.150
you're sitting down
with John here tonight,

00:48:55.150 --> 00:48:58.330
obviously a Christian believer,

00:48:58.330 --> 00:49:00.780
someone who gives evidences for God.

00:49:00.780 --> 00:49:02.847
And I know that on your show

00:49:02.847 --> 00:49:05.766
you've had people like Bishop Barron

00:49:05.766 --> 00:49:09.633
and I think Ravi Zacharias
is gonna be featured shortly.

00:49:11.030 --> 00:49:13.930
Where does this leave you on
if you like that God question?

00:49:13.930 --> 00:49:16.530
Because one level, I can
absolutely see the way

00:49:16.530 --> 00:49:18.950
in which there's a kind of the utility

00:49:18.950 --> 00:49:22.040
and a kind of sense in which meaning,

00:49:22.040 --> 00:49:24.970
we can gather meaning from
doing the religious things,

00:49:24.970 --> 00:49:26.490
the rituals and so on.

00:49:26.490 --> 00:49:28.550
But at the end of the day

00:49:28.550 --> 00:49:30.750
and I asked this of Jordan Peterson once,

00:49:30.750 --> 00:49:32.500
it was an interesting answer.

00:49:32.500 --> 00:49:33.550
Do you believe in God?

00:49:33.550 --> 00:49:36.190
Do you feel like that's
where you've got to

00:49:36.190 --> 00:49:37.490
at this point in your life?

00:49:37.490 --> 00:49:39.750
- You know it's funny
when we would do the Q&amp;As

00:49:39.750 --> 00:49:41.340
at the shows with Jordan

00:49:42.230 --> 00:49:44.060
for the first few we
would let people come up

00:49:44.060 --> 00:49:44.940
to the microphone.

00:49:44.940 --> 00:49:47.660
And what usually happens is
that people start telling

00:49:47.660 --> 00:49:49.650
their life story and they would want

00:49:49.650 --> 00:49:52.740
to get a therapy session in
front of 3,000 strangers.

00:49:52.740 --> 00:49:54.270
And it started getting very weird

00:49:54.270 --> 00:49:56.900
so we decided to better rate it.

00:49:56.900 --> 00:49:59.430
We used an app to do it and
when Jordan was on stage,

00:49:59.430 --> 00:50:01.910
basically, I would go
through the best ones

00:50:01.910 --> 00:50:03.850
and I'd find some funny
ones and some serious ones

00:50:03.850 --> 00:50:04.683
and everything else.

00:50:04.683 --> 00:50:06.060
But what always came up,

00:50:06.060 --> 00:50:08.960
no matter how many times
Jordan answered it was,

00:50:08.960 --> 00:50:10.090
do you believe in God?

00:50:10.090 --> 00:50:13.830
And he and as I'm sure he
said to you, he finds it to be

00:50:13.830 --> 00:50:16.030
sort of the most annoying
question possible.

00:50:18.379 --> 00:50:21.590
So I think I would answer it,

00:50:21.590 --> 00:50:24.620
I mean I would answer it in a
similar way that Jordan would.

00:50:24.620 --> 00:50:27.650
That look I think I've sort of laid out

00:50:27.650 --> 00:50:30.940
a set of beliefs here
that show the utility

00:50:30.940 --> 00:50:32.830
of believing in something
outside of myself

00:50:32.830 --> 00:50:35.260
and I do believe in that.

00:50:35.260 --> 00:50:38.330
So if you want to call that God,

00:50:38.330 --> 00:50:40.020
that there is something outside of me,

00:50:40.020 --> 00:50:42.910
there is something that
is connecting all of us

00:50:42.910 --> 00:50:45.810
that has nothing to do
with the material world,

00:50:45.810 --> 00:50:48.000
there is something that drives us

00:50:48.000 --> 00:50:50.120
that is the driver of humanity,

00:50:50.120 --> 00:50:53.700
that is something good, I believe that.

00:50:53.700 --> 00:50:56.458
I don't, I can't, yeah I believe that.

00:50:56.458 --> 00:50:59.307
(audience applauding)

00:50:59.307 --> 00:51:03.001
- But could you put a name on
that something at this point?

00:51:03.001 --> 00:51:03.834
(audience laughing)

00:51:03.834 --> 00:51:06.677
I told you I wanted to label
you by the end of the evening.

00:51:06.677 --> 00:51:07.933
- Ah, Jesus?

00:51:07.933 --> 00:51:12.559
(audience laughing)
(audience cheering)

00:51:12.559 --> 00:51:17.370
- I guess, I mean in a sense
I hear what you're saying.

00:51:17.370 --> 00:51:18.490
- Do I get a cookie at least?

00:51:18.490 --> 00:51:20.760
I mean, come on people
what are you doing here?

00:51:20.760 --> 00:51:22.100
(audience laughing)

00:51:22.100 --> 00:51:25.840
- I guess it's kind of like
what are the particulars though?

00:51:25.840 --> 00:51:28.790
What would that look, how is
that gonna make a difference,

00:51:28.790 --> 00:51:29.930
I suppose in your life,

00:51:29.930 --> 00:51:32.160
is there a sort of sense
in which you feel now

00:51:32.160 --> 00:51:35.290
any new obligations given
this kind of new sense

00:51:35.290 --> 00:51:36.981
that there is something beyond yourself.

00:51:36.981 --> 00:51:37.814
- You know, I think we're all

00:51:37.814 --> 00:51:38.920
sort of wired differently, right?

00:51:38.920 --> 00:51:43.920
I think some people can
really, can really flourish

00:51:44.350 --> 00:51:47.040
just sort of on their own set of ideas

00:51:47.040 --> 00:51:48.110
that they create in the world.

00:51:48.110 --> 00:51:50.100
And I think that can really
work for some people.

00:51:50.100 --> 00:51:52.660
I think some people need
some order outside of that.

00:51:52.660 --> 00:51:54.530
Some people need more of a community,

00:51:54.530 --> 00:51:55.770
some people are real loners.

00:51:55.770 --> 00:51:57.190
All of those things.

00:51:57.190 --> 00:52:00.050
I think perhaps, but I truly
mean what I said before,

00:52:00.050 --> 00:52:02.030
I'd be happy to do this
every year with you

00:52:02.030 --> 00:52:04.740
and continue these conversations

00:52:04.740 --> 00:52:07.263
and I'll continue them
on my show obviously.

00:52:09.090 --> 00:52:13.700
I think for me the
adventure of discussing this

00:52:13.700 --> 00:52:18.120
and seeing what kind of people
that I bring into my studio

00:52:18.120 --> 00:52:20.930
that interact with at events like this,

00:52:20.930 --> 00:52:23.720
what type of people I want to be around,

00:52:23.720 --> 00:52:25.723
that really is the proof.

00:52:27.187 --> 00:52:29.653
That is what,

00:52:31.380 --> 00:52:32.940
well that's what makes this, right?

00:52:32.940 --> 00:52:34.170
That's what makes this.

00:52:34.170 --> 00:52:37.440
So I don't know, so again
I can't what you're,

00:52:37.440 --> 00:52:40.130
I get the question, I
respect the question truly

00:52:40.130 --> 00:52:42.380
and it's the question, right?

00:52:42.380 --> 00:52:43.790
I mean it's like saying,
what's the meaning of life?

00:52:43.790 --> 00:52:45.083
Right, it's the big one.

00:52:45.960 --> 00:52:48.580
I would say I'm on the
adventure to finding that out

00:52:48.580 --> 00:52:50.350
and I'm really okay with that.

00:52:50.350 --> 00:52:53.400
I hope that doesn't sound dismissive

00:52:53.400 --> 00:52:57.470
of the question or like
I'm trying to evade it.

00:52:57.470 --> 00:52:59.520
I'm really not.

00:52:59.520 --> 00:53:02.610
I like, and maybe this just a function

00:53:02.610 --> 00:53:03.920
also of what I do for a living.

00:53:03.920 --> 00:53:07.190
I mean I get to sit every week with people

00:53:07.190 --> 00:53:09.500
that in most cases are smarter than me,

00:53:09.500 --> 00:53:13.410
who have spent years working
through all of these issues

00:53:13.410 --> 00:53:14.243
as you have, John.

00:53:14.243 --> 00:53:17.070
And that is an incredible
privilege that I have.

00:53:17.070 --> 00:53:20.880
So I would like to see
how far I can take that.

00:53:20.880 --> 00:53:23.532
- Sounds like we're not
gonna convert him tonight.

00:53:23.532 --> 00:53:24.540
(audience laughing)

00:53:24.540 --> 00:53:25.863
- You're doing your best.

00:53:27.023 --> 00:53:30.877
- Yeah, working overtime over
there, I'll tell ya that much.

00:53:30.877 --> 00:53:33.360
- But it's wonderful

00:53:33.360 --> 00:53:38.160
to hear an open description of a journey.

00:53:38.160 --> 00:53:42.470
And I try to think my way into this

00:53:42.470 --> 00:53:47.470
that getting around these
ideas is really big stuff.

00:53:48.570 --> 00:53:51.220
I mean coming the way you've moved

00:53:51.220 --> 00:53:54.250
from the little I've understood of it

00:53:54.250 --> 00:53:56.980
just meeting you for the first time

00:53:56.980 --> 00:53:59.230
it's most interesting to me the way

00:53:59.230 --> 00:54:01.770
that movement is going.

00:54:01.770 --> 00:54:06.770
Now from where I sit there's
another element comes into it.

00:54:07.350 --> 00:54:09.420
What I mean by that is this.

00:54:09.420 --> 00:54:13.850
There's the things that we
can think about existentially

00:54:15.013 --> 00:54:18.010
as you're doing and that's
vastly important to me as well.

00:54:18.010 --> 00:54:19.790
The kind of people you like to be with,

00:54:19.790 --> 00:54:23.140
the evidence of that, you like this,

00:54:23.140 --> 00:54:25.460
if you feel there's
something outside yourself

00:54:25.460 --> 00:54:27.540
and so on and so forth.

00:54:27.540 --> 00:54:31.650
But then it comes to
a couple of questions,

00:54:31.650 --> 00:54:36.650
One is, could it be that
something is actually personal?

00:54:39.180 --> 00:54:44.180
Now you're Jewish Hebrew
Scriptures would say exactly so

00:54:46.580 --> 00:54:50.000
because that's how
Bereshit, Genesis, starts

00:54:50.000 --> 00:54:54.060
with a God who sees,
who blesses, who speaks.

00:54:54.060 --> 00:54:57.230
And one of the most
interesting things to me,

00:54:57.230 --> 00:55:01.110
both as a scientist and a believer in God,

00:55:01.110 --> 00:55:04.503
is the simple description of creation.

00:55:04.503 --> 00:55:06.420
And God said, "Let there be light."

00:55:06.420 --> 00:55:08.100
And God said and God said.

00:55:08.100 --> 00:55:10.400
There's a sequence as you know.

00:55:10.400 --> 00:55:14.030
But the most exciting one
is the one you never hear.

00:55:14.030 --> 00:55:17.403
It's the final one and God said to them.

00:55:18.960 --> 00:55:22.630
And that opens up a whole
world of possibility.

00:55:22.630 --> 00:55:26.670
That what is being
claimed at least is that

00:55:26.670 --> 00:55:29.150
there's a God that speaks to me

00:55:30.380 --> 00:55:35.177
and that means that opens up
the possibility of revelation

00:55:36.190 --> 00:55:41.120
where it's not simply me
investigating the people I know,

00:55:41.120 --> 00:55:45.280
the things I hear, the arguments
philosophically and so on.

00:55:45.280 --> 00:55:47.665
But there's another side to it.

00:55:47.665 --> 00:55:50.790
That if this is true then
God is interested in me

00:55:50.790 --> 00:55:53.490
and he's wanting me as
a discussion partner.

00:55:53.490 --> 00:55:56.237
He's wanting to talk to me.

00:55:56.237 --> 00:55:59.120
And if one is open to that possibility

00:55:59.120 --> 00:56:04.120
it seems to me to open a
huge new dimension to this.

00:56:04.570 --> 00:56:06.980
And the more I think about that,

00:56:06.980 --> 00:56:10.630
this a word based universe scientifically

00:56:10.630 --> 00:56:13.520
and religiously in that sense.

00:56:13.520 --> 00:56:16.250
And therefore the idea

00:56:16.250 --> 00:56:19.830
that there's something
there, that's fantastic.

00:56:19.830 --> 00:56:22.230
There's more than the material world.

00:56:22.230 --> 00:56:27.120
But if that more is
personal and can speak to us

00:56:27.120 --> 00:56:29.500
it's worth testing the claim at least

00:56:29.500 --> 00:56:31.440
because it runs right through the whole

00:56:31.440 --> 00:56:33.750
of the Hebrew Scriptures
and the New Testament.

00:56:33.750 --> 00:56:38.649
That's the fundamental thing
is a speaking God, not dead.

00:56:38.649 --> 00:56:41.816
(audience applauding)

00:56:45.650 --> 00:56:48.230
- I should say, I mean we normally never

00:56:48.230 --> 00:56:51.080
on my show get to this point

00:56:51.080 --> 00:56:52.900
because most of the
time the person sitting

00:56:52.900 --> 00:56:56.040
opposite someone like John
is a confirmed atheist.

00:56:56.040 --> 00:56:58.960
And it's really, God is
always gonna be debated

00:56:58.960 --> 00:56:59.920
in the abstract.

00:56:59.920 --> 00:57:01.860
John you kind of come to
the point we're saying,

00:57:01.860 --> 00:57:04.140
well let's look at where
the God could be personal.

00:57:04.140 --> 00:57:04.973
- Yes.

00:57:04.973 --> 00:57:06.480
- Whether God could speak to us.

00:57:06.480 --> 00:57:09.970
Again I feel like we're
grilling you tonight

00:57:09.970 --> 00:57:11.470
but I guess--

00:57:11.470 --> 00:57:13.010
- No, it's fine, it's interesting.

00:57:13.010 --> 00:57:16.520
I genuinely I love this there's
nothing you could ask me

00:57:16.520 --> 00:57:18.460
I don't think that would
that would offend me or--

00:57:18.460 --> 00:57:20.490
- Well, I really I'm grateful

00:57:20.490 --> 00:57:22.050
for you being an open book tonight

00:57:22.050 --> 00:57:24.930
because I suppose my
next is question simply,

00:57:24.930 --> 00:57:25.850
could it be?

00:57:25.850 --> 00:57:28.350
Do you think that it's possible
that there could be a God

00:57:28.350 --> 00:57:30.350
who is personally interested in you,

00:57:30.350 --> 00:57:32.000
who listens to your prayers,

00:57:32.000 --> 00:57:34.630
who is interested in the
way you live your life,

00:57:34.630 --> 00:57:36.880
who wants the best for you?

00:57:36.880 --> 00:57:39.580
Is that something that's on
the table as a possibility

00:57:39.580 --> 00:57:41.560
for where your journey
might take you, I suppose.

00:57:41.560 --> 00:57:42.830
- If he walks out on stage right now,

00:57:42.830 --> 00:57:44.532
I would get baptized tomorrow, okay.

00:57:44.532 --> 00:57:46.240
(audience laughing)

00:57:46.240 --> 00:57:48.770
Like if this is like a Maury Povich

00:57:48.770 --> 00:57:50.710
and here he is everybody!

00:57:50.710 --> 00:57:53.660
(audience laughing)

00:57:53.660 --> 00:57:56.800
Yes, well my basic answer would be, yes.

00:57:56.800 --> 00:57:59.020
Why would I rule that out?

00:57:59.020 --> 00:58:00.370
Why would I rule that out?

00:58:00.370 --> 00:58:02.070
Why, as you said that so eloquently,

00:58:02.070 --> 00:58:03.293
would I be like, no.

00:58:06.210 --> 00:58:08.410
That just doesn't stand to reason.

00:58:08.410 --> 00:58:11.310
And that again goes back to why I said

00:58:11.310 --> 00:58:13.050
I love having these conversations.

00:58:13.050 --> 00:58:15.700
And I don't even, though
this a little different

00:58:15.700 --> 00:58:19.790
for what you do usually,
I don't take offense by it

00:58:19.790 --> 00:58:20.630
or anything like that.

00:58:20.630 --> 00:58:22.347
Like that's interesting to me.

00:58:22.347 --> 00:58:27.347
But and by the way I think
that this sort of where Jews

00:58:27.640 --> 00:58:30.120
maybe have done something
a little bit differently,

00:58:30.120 --> 00:58:33.330
where it always has been
about this sort of battle

00:58:33.330 --> 00:58:34.963
about what God is?

00:58:35.950 --> 00:58:38.200
You talk about science and mathematics.

00:58:38.200 --> 00:58:41.290
You can go to most of the Jewish hospitals

00:58:41.290 --> 00:58:44.210
in New York City where
they have the best doctors

00:58:44.210 --> 00:58:46.700
in the entire world, many
of them are Orthodox Jews

00:58:46.700 --> 00:58:50.210
who actually won't press
the elevator on Shabbat

00:58:50.210 --> 00:58:51.500
because they don't want to
use electricity on Shabbat.

00:58:51.500 --> 00:58:53.440
So they have the elevators

00:58:53.440 --> 00:58:56.350
that go to every floor on Saturdays.

00:58:56.350 --> 00:58:57.750
Now from an outsider perspective

00:58:57.750 --> 00:58:59.110
you weren't really
thinking about it that way,

00:58:59.110 --> 00:59:00.780
you'd go well that's completely crazy.

00:59:00.780 --> 00:59:04.540
How are these people of
science and math, doctors,

00:59:04.540 --> 00:59:08.790
why would they possibly care
in this crazy superstition

00:59:08.790 --> 00:59:13.360
if you come from that
discipline of science and math.

00:59:13.360 --> 00:59:16.750
And yet those people don't
find it to be in conflict.

00:59:16.750 --> 00:59:20.420
So I don't see any of
this in conflict actually.

00:59:20.420 --> 00:59:23.030
If anything, I feel like
this really what it's about.

00:59:23.030 --> 00:59:28.000
This ability to play, because
I know that there are plenty

00:59:28.000 --> 00:59:30.620
of you guys out there
that are at some level

00:59:30.620 --> 00:59:32.330
of where I'm at with some of this stuff.

00:59:32.330 --> 00:59:34.730
It's not like everyone that
walks in these doors is going

00:59:34.730 --> 00:59:37.650
this absolutely what I believe
and we believe the same thing

00:59:37.650 --> 00:59:38.810
and I want to convert everybody

00:59:38.810 --> 00:59:39.840
to believe in the same thing I do.

00:59:39.840 --> 00:59:40.960
I just, I don't believe that.

00:59:40.960 --> 00:59:43.042
I'm not gonna poll you don't worry.

00:59:43.042 --> 00:59:44.030
(audience laughing)

00:59:44.030 --> 00:59:46.343
But I know that's true.

00:59:47.210 --> 00:59:49.000
And we're all on those journeys together.

00:59:49.000 --> 00:59:52.940
So of course I don't dismiss
could God be a personal,

00:59:52.940 --> 00:59:57.367
you know do we all have
that piece of us behind us

00:59:57.367 --> 01:00:01.630
that knows what's right when
you make a bad choice in life.

01:00:01.630 --> 01:00:03.220
You have those, yeah, I
shouldn't have that drink

01:00:03.220 --> 01:00:05.410
or shouldn't do this or whatever it is.

01:00:05.410 --> 01:00:07.220
I don't know is that a
personal relationship with God

01:00:07.220 --> 01:00:08.150
when you have that other thing

01:00:08.150 --> 01:00:10.130
or some would say is that
just the voice in your head,

01:00:10.130 --> 01:00:12.160
what is that, what is that thing?

01:00:12.160 --> 01:00:14.823
I mean philosophers have
been debating this forever.

01:00:16.490 --> 01:00:17.960
- I think that's a really great place

01:00:17.960 --> 01:00:20.800
just to draw this part of
the conversation to a close.

01:00:20.800 --> 01:00:21.745
- Thank God.

01:00:21.745 --> 01:00:22.578
(audience laughing)

01:00:22.578 --> 01:00:24.900
Whoa, all right, okay.

01:00:24.900 --> 01:00:26.184
Literally, I mean that.

01:00:26.184 --> 01:00:27.740
- Let's give a round of
applause to John and Dave.

01:00:27.740 --> 01:00:32.325
(audience applauding)
(audience cheering)

01:00:32.325 --> 01:00:34.908
(upbeat music)

01:00:36.409 --> 01:00:39.150
(audience applauding)

01:00:39.150 --> 01:00:40.936
Oh there's a lot for you I'm afraid, Dave.

01:00:40.936 --> 01:00:43.090
(audience laughing)

01:00:43.090 --> 01:00:45.713
- Can I text in a couple
questions myself right now?

01:00:48.070 --> 01:00:50.700
- I mean there is one very obvious one

01:00:50.700 --> 01:00:52.840
which someone wants to
ask and if you could

01:00:52.840 --> 01:00:54.530
this could be a whole
evening for you, John,

01:00:54.530 --> 01:00:56.480
but someone simply asks

01:00:56.480 --> 01:00:58.960
what for you would be
the key evidences for God

01:00:58.960 --> 01:01:01.870
if you were in a conversation
with someone about that?

01:01:01.870 --> 01:01:04.430
- It depends entirely whom I'm talking to

01:01:05.400 --> 01:01:08.493
because it seems to me there
are two kinds of evidence.

01:01:08.493 --> 01:01:11.650
There's the objective kind of evidence.

01:01:11.650 --> 01:01:15.130
That is from the scientific point of view,

01:01:15.130 --> 01:01:17.050
looking at the universe, the beginning,

01:01:17.050 --> 01:01:20.520
the fine-tuning of the universe
and all those pointers,

01:01:20.520 --> 01:01:22.380
the very doing of science that point

01:01:22.380 --> 01:01:24.685
to an intelligence behind the universe.

01:01:24.685 --> 01:01:26.350
That would be one set.

01:01:26.350 --> 01:01:31.350
The second set would be the
whole revelation through Christ

01:01:32.170 --> 01:01:35.910
and who he is and what he did
and all that kind of thing.

01:01:35.910 --> 01:01:39.330
And central to it of
course would be the fact

01:01:39.330 --> 01:01:43.430
that I believe the death
problem has been solved

01:01:43.430 --> 01:01:46.840
in the sense that at the
heart of Christianity

01:01:46.840 --> 01:01:49.640
there's not only a
death but a resurrection

01:01:49.640 --> 01:01:52.180
which validates the
significance of the death.

01:01:52.180 --> 01:01:55.850
So I want to go into the what
I believe are the evidences

01:01:55.850 --> 01:01:58.960
for the resurrection
historically and so on.

01:01:58.960 --> 01:02:01.720
And then finally there's
the existential side.

01:02:01.720 --> 01:02:05.217
Many people say to me
you can't be a Christian

01:02:05.217 --> 01:02:09.800
and a scientist because
Christianity is not testable.

01:02:09.800 --> 01:02:12.033
And I say of course it's testable.

01:02:13.897 --> 01:02:17.960
And to be blunt about that, very briefly,

01:02:17.960 --> 01:02:21.490
Christ makes certain
claims that if we trust him

01:02:21.490 --> 01:02:26.150
he'll give us peace with God, forgiveness,

01:02:26.150 --> 01:02:27.690
new life, a new par.

01:02:27.690 --> 01:02:29.390
You can test that.

01:02:29.390 --> 01:02:32.300
And you know in my life,
which has been quite long,

01:02:32.300 --> 01:02:34.650
I've met many people and you meet them

01:02:34.650 --> 01:02:36.850
and there maybe in the midst

01:02:36.850 --> 01:02:39.780
of a broken relationship,
narcotic dependence,

01:02:39.780 --> 01:02:44.170
all kinds of stuff and you
meet them six months later

01:02:44.170 --> 01:02:46.647
and they're completely different.

01:02:46.647 --> 01:02:48.180
And you say, "What's happened to you?"

01:02:48.180 --> 01:02:50.460
Now they may express
it in different terms.

01:02:50.460 --> 01:02:52.620
They may say, "Well I became a Christian,"

01:02:52.620 --> 01:02:55.100
or "I was born again,"
or "I was converted."

01:02:55.100 --> 01:02:57.950
But when you see that as I
have done again and again

01:02:57.950 --> 01:03:00.880
not only in my own life
and those around me

01:03:00.880 --> 01:03:02.750
but in people around you,

01:03:02.750 --> 01:03:04.930
you add two and two and you get four.

01:03:04.930 --> 01:03:09.010
And I wouldn't sit here for
a nanosecond as a Christian

01:03:09.010 --> 01:03:13.650
if I didn't believe it can
be tested in real experience.

01:03:13.650 --> 01:03:16.500
If it doesn't work then it's very suspect.

01:03:16.500 --> 01:03:19.440
So that is the fastest
answer I can give to that.

01:03:19.440 --> 01:03:22.183
- Thank you very much
ready for the answers.

01:03:22.183 --> 01:03:23.480
(audience applauding)

01:03:23.480 --> 01:03:25.230
That's a lot of thinking boiled down

01:03:25.230 --> 01:03:27.330
into a very brief answer.

01:03:27.330 --> 01:03:29.527
Question for you, Dave, has come in

01:03:29.527 --> 01:03:34.527
and it's what do you find
unbelievable about Christianity?

01:03:35.050 --> 01:03:37.630
So I guess the question is,
okay you're on a journey,

01:03:37.630 --> 01:03:41.270
you're willing to be open
to that potential answer

01:03:41.270 --> 01:03:43.260
what are the main objections at this point

01:03:43.260 --> 01:03:45.460
that you would have to kind of embracing

01:03:45.460 --> 01:03:46.660
that kind of a view?

01:03:47.815 --> 01:03:48.970
- Well I suppose it's a good question

01:03:48.970 --> 01:03:51.810
because I don't have a
particularly good answer for it.

01:03:51.810 --> 01:03:54.680
So I think we could probably
do this one pretty quickly.

01:03:54.680 --> 01:03:56.960
I mean, I don't, there's no...

01:03:58.200 --> 01:04:02.580
Well other than what
would be a generic answer

01:04:02.580 --> 01:04:03.530
about any religion.

01:04:04.419 --> 01:04:05.870
Let's say some sort of
like true leap of faith

01:04:05.870 --> 01:04:09.230
to ultimately say this
whether it's Christianity

01:04:09.230 --> 01:04:11.030
or Judaism or whatever you want to say,

01:04:11.030 --> 01:04:13.270
whatever religion or set of
ideas we're talking about.

01:04:13.270 --> 01:04:17.600
Beyond that full jump I don't have any,

01:04:17.600 --> 01:04:20.380
there's nothing, there's
nothing that's been said up here

01:04:20.380 --> 01:04:22.520
there's nothing that John has talked about

01:04:22.520 --> 01:04:24.070
or than any of the religious thinkers

01:04:24.070 --> 01:04:26.250
that I've had on recently.

01:04:26.250 --> 01:04:27.980
The conversation that I
had with Ravi Zacharias,

01:04:27.980 --> 01:04:28.813
it's going up next week

01:04:28.813 --> 01:04:30.490
I think is one of the best interviews

01:04:30.490 --> 01:04:31.890
that I've ever been part of.

01:04:31.890 --> 01:04:33.640
There was nothing that he said there

01:04:33.640 --> 01:04:35.360
in the course of that hour and a half

01:04:35.360 --> 01:04:36.810
that I found objectionable

01:04:36.810 --> 01:04:41.420
or I found completely incongruent

01:04:41.420 --> 01:04:43.030
with my set of beliefs, let's say.

01:04:43.030 --> 01:04:44.260
So I don't know that I have,

01:04:44.260 --> 01:04:47.510
there's no answer for
that in that I'm willing

01:04:47.510 --> 01:04:49.313
to keep continue that conversation.

01:04:50.600 --> 01:04:53.500
- A few people have asked a
follow-up question effectively

01:04:54.893 --> 01:04:59.893
which is I mean do, it's
phrased in a few different ways,

01:05:00.095 --> 01:05:00.928
so I'll try and--

01:05:00.928 --> 01:05:01.914
- Is it hot here?

01:05:01.914 --> 01:05:03.798
(audience laughing)

01:05:03.798 --> 01:05:05.780
- It kind of goes to the
heart of Christianity though

01:05:05.780 --> 01:05:09.300
which is what do you think
about the central claim

01:05:09.300 --> 01:05:10.630
that Jesus rose from the dead.

01:05:10.630 --> 01:05:12.830
Because that at the end of the day is

01:05:12.830 --> 01:05:16.290
at the heart of the faith John holds.

01:05:16.290 --> 01:05:18.870
Is that something you would
be open to investigating

01:05:18.870 --> 01:05:20.230
to kind of you know seeing

01:05:20.230 --> 01:05:22.780
whether there's anything in that claim?

01:05:22.780 --> 01:05:25.139
- Yeah I, look, I think I don't know

01:05:25.139 --> 01:05:26.710
how many of these types of
questions there's going to be.

01:05:26.710 --> 01:05:28.827
- [Justin] I won't do
too many more of them.

01:05:28.827 --> 01:05:30.443
- But yes I can't,

01:05:31.693 --> 01:05:33.300
I wish I could think of
a better way to say it.

01:05:33.300 --> 01:05:36.840
But I am completely open
to these discussions

01:05:36.840 --> 01:05:39.440
as they have, your answer
actually John was perfect.

01:05:39.440 --> 01:05:41.690
That if these things weren't testable

01:05:41.690 --> 01:05:44.130
in that you did not see the evidence

01:05:44.130 --> 01:05:47.200
that people were living better
lives, more fulfilled lives,

01:05:47.200 --> 01:05:48.510
happier lives, all those things,

01:05:48.510 --> 01:05:50.130
that you wouldn't as a man of science,

01:05:50.130 --> 01:05:52.140
you could not sit up here.

01:05:52.140 --> 01:05:53.700
That's a really beautiful answer

01:05:53.700 --> 01:05:57.867
that feels to me to be
complete as a person of faith

01:05:57.867 --> 01:05:59.770
and a person of science, right?

01:05:59.770 --> 01:06:01.790
And I think that would be a worldview

01:06:01.790 --> 01:06:03.040
that I could prescribe to

01:06:03.040 --> 01:06:04.730
and that is that actually is a worldview

01:06:04.730 --> 01:06:06.160
that I prescribe to.

01:06:06.160 --> 01:06:09.790
And that doesn't exclude
the fact that very early on

01:06:09.790 --> 01:06:11.900
I said that at the micro level

01:06:11.900 --> 01:06:13.411
some of my best friends are atheists

01:06:13.411 --> 01:06:16.710
and they are deeply
moral, good, decent people

01:06:17.831 --> 01:06:20.170
and you've debated many of them.

01:06:20.170 --> 01:06:24.350
so there's no, there's nothing

01:06:24.350 --> 01:06:27.220
that is so separate, let's say.

01:06:27.220 --> 01:06:32.220
- I mean, I promise no more
grilling you on faith questions.

01:06:34.351 --> 01:06:37.983
But for John, kind of the
opposite question coming in.

01:06:39.099 --> 01:06:41.670
Is there, have you ever
questioned if there is a God?

01:06:41.670 --> 01:06:45.510
And another person asks, whether
you've ever been presented

01:06:45.510 --> 01:06:47.160
with an objection that
you found difficult?

01:06:47.160 --> 01:06:48.512
- Oh, can I do that one?

01:06:48.512 --> 01:06:50.135
(audience laughing)

01:06:50.135 --> 01:06:51.218
Come on, man.

01:06:53.770 --> 01:06:55.330
- Of course I have.

01:06:55.330 --> 01:07:00.330
You see, this interest me
because I spent my whole life

01:07:01.600 --> 01:07:04.970
not only questioning
myself but exposing myself

01:07:04.970 --> 01:07:08.260
to some of the most powerful
questioners in the world

01:07:08.260 --> 01:07:10.180
who are against my viewpoint.

01:07:10.180 --> 01:07:14.010
And the reason for doing that
is partially the Freudian one.

01:07:14.010 --> 01:07:16.520
The accusation that was made
against me at Cambridge.

01:07:16.520 --> 01:07:18.482
You believe because your parents believe

01:07:18.482 --> 01:07:19.315
and your grandparents believe

01:07:19.315 --> 01:07:21.850
and it's Irish genetics, end of story.

01:07:21.850 --> 01:07:22.790
(audience laughing)

01:07:22.790 --> 01:07:27.680
So I have questioned
everything in that sense

01:07:27.680 --> 01:07:31.850
and it's through that my certainty comes.

01:07:31.850 --> 01:07:34.490
You see, the word faith
has flicked in there

01:07:34.490 --> 01:07:37.130
to this conversation and
we need to be careful

01:07:37.130 --> 01:07:38.610
what we mean by that.

01:07:38.610 --> 01:07:41.690
By faith I don't mean
a leap into the dark.

01:07:41.690 --> 01:07:44.520
I mean a step into the
light that's evidence-based

01:07:44.520 --> 01:07:48.960
and it's exactly the same
kind of commitment in science.

01:07:48.960 --> 01:07:51.240
We believe certain
things about the universe

01:07:51.240 --> 01:07:53.270
because we've evidence.

01:07:53.270 --> 01:07:57.340
Now in Christianity it
is exactly parallel.

01:07:57.340 --> 01:08:02.340
Real faith is a commitment,
a step based on evidence

01:08:03.850 --> 01:08:08.190
and of course there's a personal
dimension to Christianity

01:08:08.190 --> 01:08:09.250
that doesn't occur

01:08:09.250 --> 01:08:11.770
when you're believing Einstein's
equations for example.

01:08:11.770 --> 01:08:14.810
It's not quite the same
as trusting a person.

01:08:14.810 --> 01:08:19.810
But still it's got to be
evidence-based or else is blind.

01:08:20.860 --> 01:08:23.510
And blind faith is extremely dangerous,

01:08:23.510 --> 01:08:26.490
particularly in the sphere of religion.

01:08:26.490 --> 01:08:31.490
So asking questions all
the time is a process

01:08:32.850 --> 01:08:34.730
that I discover in the Bible itself

01:08:34.730 --> 01:08:37.530
which is one of the things
that makes it credible.

01:08:37.530 --> 01:08:39.850
Jesus was always asking questions

01:08:39.850 --> 01:08:41.617
and he didn't always answer them

01:08:41.617 --> 01:08:44.960
and his disciples were asking questions.

01:08:44.960 --> 01:08:48.930
My great intellectual hero was Socrates

01:08:48.930 --> 01:08:51.580
who was forced to commit suicide

01:08:51.580 --> 01:08:53.980
because he corrupted the youth of Athens

01:08:53.980 --> 01:08:57.010
which meant that he taught
kids to ask questions

01:08:57.010 --> 01:08:59.000
their parents couldn't answer.

01:08:59.000 --> 01:09:01.540
Which is usually what kids do.

01:09:01.540 --> 01:09:05.670
But it seems to me that
it's very important.

01:09:05.670 --> 01:09:09.510
My whole process of relating
to people, as Dave's is,

01:09:09.510 --> 01:09:11.320
is asking them questions

01:09:11.320 --> 01:09:13.750
and getting them to open themselves.

01:09:13.750 --> 01:09:18.750
So yes, absolutely this
is the way the thing works

01:09:19.070 --> 01:09:21.430
and that is where assurance comes.

01:09:21.430 --> 01:09:26.430
It's fear that stops us
questioning the propositions

01:09:26.630 --> 01:09:31.196
or the commitments of what we believe.

01:09:31.196 --> 01:09:33.960
And I want to break
through that fear barrier

01:09:33.960 --> 01:09:36.223
and get into a proper public discourse.

01:09:37.990 --> 01:09:42.850
- There's a question here which
relates to a story you told

01:09:42.850 --> 01:09:44.670
on your podcast of being

01:09:44.670 --> 01:09:47.980
I think at a John Peterson lecture night

01:09:47.980 --> 01:09:49.230
and you met a couple

01:09:49.230 --> 01:09:52.080
who had both been
through a difficult time.

01:09:52.080 --> 01:09:53.630
Perhaps you can tell the story.

01:09:54.700 --> 01:09:56.200
I think a miscarriage is involved

01:09:56.200 --> 01:09:58.660
and there were difficulties in the family

01:09:58.660 --> 01:10:02.480
and the way you responded
was the interesting

01:10:02.480 --> 01:10:04.087
perhaps part of that story as well.

01:10:04.087 --> 01:10:04.920
Do you want to tell the story

01:10:04.920 --> 01:10:07.063
and just maybe dig into that a bit

01:10:07.063 --> 01:10:08.990
because the person asks
that they'd just like

01:10:08.990 --> 01:10:11.000
to hear a little bit more about that.

01:10:11.000 --> 01:10:11.833
- Yeah, you know it's so interesting

01:10:11.833 --> 01:10:12.666
that someone's asking this

01:10:12.666 --> 01:10:14.640
because I actually don't remember telling

01:10:14.640 --> 01:10:15.760
this story publicly.

01:10:15.760 --> 01:10:18.580
But I must have said it
publicly at some point.

01:10:18.580 --> 01:10:19.413
I don't remember.

01:10:19.413 --> 01:10:23.930
We were in the UK, I don't
remember what city we were in,

01:10:23.930 --> 01:10:25.960
but a couple came up to me before the show

01:10:25.960 --> 01:10:28.790
and they both looked very,

01:10:28.790 --> 01:10:29.980
they were just sort of distraught.

01:10:29.980 --> 01:10:31.758
You know sometimes you
people come up to you

01:10:31.758 --> 01:10:32.591
and you could just see it in their face,

01:10:32.591 --> 01:10:34.500
you could feel in their body language

01:10:34.500 --> 01:10:36.140
and they started talking to me.

01:10:36.140 --> 01:10:41.140
And basically it turned out
that he, oh, it was young couple

01:10:42.090 --> 01:10:45.350
I mean they were in their
maybe late 20s, early 30s

01:10:45.350 --> 01:10:48.240
and the guy had just found out

01:10:48.240 --> 01:10:50.870
that he had like stage four cancer

01:10:50.870 --> 01:10:52.720
and didn't have much time to live.

01:10:52.720 --> 01:10:55.310
And then, yeah, I mean
that you can't believe

01:10:55.310 --> 01:10:56.143
it is actually real.

01:10:56.143 --> 01:10:59.700
The woman found out that she was pregnant,

01:10:59.700 --> 01:11:02.900
she was a few months pregnant
and the fetus had died

01:11:02.900 --> 01:11:04.690
and she was gonna have to have it removed

01:11:04.690 --> 01:11:06.590
like the next day or something like that.

01:11:06.590 --> 01:11:11.090
I mean like unimaginably
horrific circumstances.

01:11:11.090 --> 01:11:12.950
And they said this to me and then said

01:11:12.950 --> 01:11:15.940
that Jordan's helped
them get a little meaning

01:11:15.940 --> 01:11:18.307
over the last few weeks as
they've been going to doctors

01:11:18.307 --> 01:11:20.000
and all of these things.

01:11:20.000 --> 01:11:22.130
And I think the reason that
the person's mentioning

01:11:22.130 --> 01:11:23.680
that I bring it up is the only thing

01:11:23.680 --> 01:11:27.917
that I could say was, "God
bless you, God bless you, guys."

01:11:27.917 --> 01:11:28.790
That's what I said.

01:11:28.790 --> 01:11:30.940
I had, there was nothing else,

01:11:30.940 --> 01:11:32.370
there was just nothing else I could say.

01:11:32.370 --> 01:11:33.967
What can I possibly say,

01:11:33.967 --> 01:11:35.977
"I'm sorry to hear about
what you're going through."

01:11:35.977 --> 01:11:39.020
You know, I hugged them I tried
to just kind of stand there

01:11:39.020 --> 01:11:39.893
and be with them.

01:11:40.930 --> 01:11:43.610
But that happened to be
what popped up in my brain.

01:11:43.610 --> 01:11:46.150
So if that, in a weird way,

01:11:46.150 --> 01:11:47.690
if that is evidence of something,

01:11:47.690 --> 01:11:52.130
if that is that when
faced with unimaginable,

01:11:52.130 --> 01:11:56.030
I mean no one can possibly
imagine the level of horror

01:11:56.030 --> 01:11:57.193
of that situation.

01:11:58.060 --> 01:12:00.360
I hope I'm getting it
fully technically right.

01:12:01.950 --> 01:12:04.450
That's what I had left I would say.

01:12:04.450 --> 01:12:06.547
- It was the only words
that you felt made sense.

01:12:06.547 --> 01:12:08.935
- Can I make a comment?
- Yes, please do.

01:12:08.935 --> 01:12:11.759
- Because I didn't answer your question.

01:12:11.759 --> 01:12:13.543
And one of your questions to me was

01:12:14.740 --> 01:12:18.460
do you make objections that
you find difficult to answer.

01:12:18.460 --> 01:12:21.100
And I do and that's one.

01:12:21.100 --> 01:12:26.080
The problem of moral evil and pain,

01:12:26.080 --> 01:12:28.250
which is what you talked about,

01:12:28.250 --> 01:12:31.593
is the hardest question
that any of us face.

01:12:32.870 --> 01:12:36.740
And it has been a very
important part of my life

01:12:36.740 --> 01:12:39.310
to try and think that through.

01:12:39.310 --> 01:12:42.120
I arrived in Christchurch,
New Zealand two days

01:12:42.120 --> 01:12:46.380
after the earthquake and I
had to cancel all my talks

01:12:46.380 --> 01:12:49.287
and replace them with "Earthquake, Why?"

01:12:50.500 --> 01:12:52.460
Now that's not easy to deal with.

01:12:52.460 --> 01:12:57.460
Now, the atheists say, "Well
it's just the way the world is.

01:12:57.957 --> 01:13:00.960
"There is no God, that's
just what you'd expect."

01:13:00.960 --> 01:13:03.500
But you see, if you abolish God

01:13:03.500 --> 01:13:05.210
and I'm not gonna go into details in this

01:13:05.210 --> 01:13:07.390
but it's hugely important.

01:13:07.390 --> 01:13:10.590
If you abolish God actually
you can make the problem worse

01:13:10.590 --> 01:13:13.300
because there's now no hope.

01:13:13.300 --> 01:13:16.200
If you bring God into the equation

01:13:16.200 --> 01:13:18.920
from the Christian perspective

01:13:18.920 --> 01:13:21.253
there is the hope of the Resurrection.

01:13:22.100 --> 01:13:25.030
And not going into it in detail,

01:13:25.030 --> 01:13:27.950
I want to say two big
things to this problem.

01:13:27.950 --> 01:13:31.500
They're not answers, that would
be simplistic and trivial.

01:13:31.500 --> 01:13:34.360
But they're are a window
into a possibility.

01:13:34.360 --> 01:13:37.250
And the window into the
possibility is this.

01:13:37.250 --> 01:13:42.250
If it really is true that's
God incarnate on the cross

01:13:42.880 --> 01:13:44.450
what does it mean?

01:13:44.450 --> 01:13:46.380
Well at the very least it means

01:13:46.380 --> 01:13:49.610
that God has not remained
distant from human suffering.

01:13:49.610 --> 01:13:52.037
He has become part of it.

01:13:52.037 --> 01:13:56.180
And so your God bless you,
I think it's a huge layers

01:13:56.180 --> 01:13:58.210
of meaning underlying it.

01:13:58.210 --> 01:13:59.600
That's the first thing.

01:13:59.600 --> 01:14:03.730
And the fact that Jesus
rose from the dead means

01:14:03.730 --> 01:14:06.440
that ultimately there is real hope.

01:14:06.440 --> 01:14:08.780
Why, because one of the things

01:14:08.780 --> 01:14:11.100
the early Christians preached was

01:14:11.100 --> 01:14:14.570
that the resurrection
guaranteed a final judgment.

01:14:14.570 --> 01:14:17.330
And we don't like that
idea, but think about it.

01:14:17.330 --> 01:14:21.003
If there's going to be an
utterly fair assessment,

01:14:23.750 --> 01:14:28.260
although as I said I've been
to Auschwitz many times,

01:14:28.260 --> 01:14:30.200
if there's going to be a judgment

01:14:31.100 --> 01:14:36.100
that deals with these things
that gives me a window of hope.

01:14:36.440 --> 01:14:40.190
And so, therefore, I think
this the hardest problem

01:14:40.190 --> 01:14:42.160
for anybody and any philosophy

01:14:42.160 --> 01:14:44.233
in any world for you nobody escapes it.

01:14:46.255 --> 01:14:48.410
- A question for you this time, John.

01:14:48.410 --> 01:14:51.494
And maybe you might want
comment on it as well, Dave.

01:14:51.494 --> 01:14:53.460
But someone asked what
does it take for a person

01:14:53.460 --> 01:14:56.990
to move from an abstract
intellectual belief in God

01:14:56.990 --> 01:15:00.730
to this personal experiential
faith which you talked about.

01:15:00.730 --> 01:15:03.170
- Well that's assuming that they start

01:15:03.170 --> 01:15:05.090
with an abstract faith.

01:15:05.090 --> 01:15:07.140
Now people come different ways.

01:15:07.140 --> 01:15:08.360
Our journeys, and that's why

01:15:08.360 --> 01:15:11.000
it's so interesting talking about journey

01:15:11.000 --> 01:15:13.070
and I haven't finished my journey yet.

01:15:13.070 --> 01:15:16.800
There are lots of questions
I've got, things unresolved.

01:15:16.800 --> 01:15:20.260
I started by the existential
side, the personal side,

01:15:20.260 --> 01:15:21.860
I saw it was real.

01:15:21.860 --> 01:15:25.280
But then as a child I was
taught to ask the questions,

01:15:25.280 --> 01:15:27.320
so the intellectual side

01:15:27.320 --> 01:15:30.460
and the practical side walked hand in hand

01:15:30.460 --> 01:15:32.010
throughout my life.

01:15:32.010 --> 01:15:37.010
And some people come to faith in Christ

01:15:37.930 --> 01:15:41.290
because they've been
befriended by somebody

01:15:41.290 --> 01:15:45.280
that shows a real friendship
and it's existential.

01:15:45.280 --> 01:15:48.470
Others come because they've
been challenged intellectually.

01:15:48.470 --> 01:15:52.410
I was speaking in Germany a week ago

01:15:52.410 --> 01:15:57.410
and speaking with a
very intellectual person

01:15:57.560 --> 01:16:01.210
and what started him off in
his journey was at school

01:16:01.210 --> 01:16:05.360
he was grown up in a, he grew up in a home

01:16:05.360 --> 01:16:07.690
where all his father said about everything

01:16:07.690 --> 01:16:11.860
was everything's lies, that
was his, everything's lies.

01:16:11.860 --> 01:16:13.720
Total skepticism.

01:16:13.720 --> 01:16:16.413
And he had a school
friend, they sat together

01:16:16.413 --> 01:16:18.740
and the school friend said to him one day,

01:16:18.740 --> 01:16:21.687
he said, "Jesus Christ
rose from the dead."

01:16:22.699 --> 01:16:24.966
And this chap thought, "What?"

01:16:24.966 --> 01:16:26.440
And he started to investigate it.

01:16:26.440 --> 01:16:30.320
And that led him to become
an ancient historian.

01:16:30.320 --> 01:16:33.310
And now in Germany he'll fill a hall

01:16:33.310 --> 01:16:36.497
with 5,000 people talking about

01:16:36.497 --> 01:16:39.120
how he became a Christian from skepticism.

01:16:39.120 --> 01:16:41.943
So the intellectual side got him first.

01:16:43.330 --> 01:16:48.080
So you can't generalize at all.

01:16:48.080 --> 01:16:50.850
But I think in that sense

01:16:50.850 --> 01:16:53.243
if a person is just cerebral about it

01:16:53.243 --> 01:16:54.880
they haven't understood it

01:16:54.880 --> 01:16:57.200
because Christ does make demands.

01:16:57.200 --> 01:16:58.610
There's a relational thing,

01:16:58.610 --> 01:17:01.630
there are moral dimensions to it.

01:17:01.630 --> 01:17:06.450
And so it needs to move,
how shall I put it?

01:17:06.450 --> 01:17:08.770
I'm worried about the distinction

01:17:08.770 --> 01:17:12.850
because even when we're
emotional about something

01:17:12.850 --> 01:17:16.100
the emotions are
precipitated by our thoughts.

01:17:16.100 --> 01:17:19.000
You can't separate thought and emotion.

01:17:19.000 --> 01:17:22.900
But I think the important
thing is that since,

01:17:22.900 --> 01:17:24.560
let me put it this way.

01:17:24.560 --> 01:17:27.570
God is not simply a philosophical theory.

01:17:27.570 --> 01:17:30.220
He's a person and therefore our response

01:17:30.220 --> 01:17:32.470
to persons is different from a response

01:17:32.470 --> 01:17:34.233
to say a mathematical equation.

01:17:35.170 --> 01:17:40.170
And we need to take the various dimensions

01:17:40.560 --> 01:17:42.440
and aspects into account.

01:17:42.440 --> 01:17:46.100
So I'd simply want to
encourage a person like that

01:17:46.100 --> 01:17:51.040
to think about, well, what
does Christianity mean

01:17:51.040 --> 01:17:52.943
that you should be doing.

01:17:54.460 --> 01:17:56.790
But since it's an abstract question,

01:17:56.790 --> 01:17:59.480
I don't know the person,
everybody's different.

01:17:59.480 --> 01:18:01.503
- I'm tossing it over to you, Dave.

01:18:01.503 --> 01:18:03.040
There's a sense in which, you know,

01:18:03.040 --> 01:18:04.850
we've talked a lot about
Jordan Peterson this evening.

01:18:04.850 --> 01:18:07.020
But when I see him on stage,

01:18:07.020 --> 01:18:09.330
he's not afraid of getting emotional.

01:18:09.330 --> 01:18:12.270
In fact, he quite frequently
is close to tears very often

01:18:12.270 --> 01:18:15.780
when it's talking about
the search for meaning,

01:18:15.780 --> 01:18:17.980
when he's engaging with
where people are at

01:18:17.980 --> 01:18:19.930
in their struggles and so on.

01:18:19.930 --> 01:18:21.730
And in that sense he's quite the antidote

01:18:21.730 --> 01:18:23.860
to the kind of very coldly
rational kind of approach

01:18:23.860 --> 01:18:27.310
that many sort of big thinkers
often take to these issues.

01:18:27.310 --> 01:18:29.340
Is that something that's attracted you,

01:18:29.340 --> 01:18:32.020
I don't know, to the
way in which he engages

01:18:32.020 --> 01:18:36.550
with that kind of aspect of
reality in a very personal way

01:18:36.550 --> 01:18:39.100
in a sense in which he
approaches these kinds of issues?

01:18:39.100 --> 01:18:40.870
- Yeah, well I can answer that two ways.

01:18:40.870 --> 01:18:43.560
First, not specifically with Jordan,

01:18:43.560 --> 01:18:47.380
but I think one of the
reasons that the YouTube game

01:18:47.380 --> 01:18:50.030
and the podcast game and
all of the digital stuff

01:18:50.030 --> 01:18:52.820
and all of this new set of personalities

01:18:52.820 --> 01:18:55.970
that you guys all watch
and consume and listen to,

01:18:55.970 --> 01:18:57.040
the reason it's working is

01:18:57.040 --> 01:19:00.010
because these people are being people.

01:19:00.010 --> 01:19:01.440
These are actual people.

01:19:01.440 --> 01:19:03.890
I mean the person that you see

01:19:03.890 --> 01:19:05.790
when I'm doing something on YouTube,

01:19:05.790 --> 01:19:07.860
that's this guy that's right
in front of you right now.

01:19:07.860 --> 01:19:09.470
I happen to be on stage with a mic

01:19:09.470 --> 01:19:11.850
but I'm the same person
when I step down there

01:19:11.850 --> 01:19:12.970
and talk to you guys.

01:19:12.970 --> 01:19:14.270
Jordan is the same person.

01:19:14.270 --> 01:19:15.630
As I said, he lived those 12 rules.

01:19:15.630 --> 01:19:17.880
He's the same exact guy, right?

01:19:17.880 --> 01:19:20.250
There's a little something
when there's a camera there

01:19:20.250 --> 01:19:24.450
I forget what the sight,
there's a theory, you know,

01:19:24.450 --> 01:19:25.730
that you all, when a camera's on

01:19:25.730 --> 01:19:27.880
you turn it on like 5% or something.

01:19:27.880 --> 01:19:30.470
But I think what's happened
over the last 20 or so years

01:19:30.470 --> 01:19:32.980
with cable news is that
everyone on television became

01:19:32.980 --> 01:19:36.334
so incredibly fake and over prepared

01:19:36.334 --> 01:19:40.330
and really ridiculously robotic

01:19:40.330 --> 01:19:41.810
that there is a breath of fresh air

01:19:41.810 --> 01:19:44.870
when people just actually are decent,

01:19:44.870 --> 01:19:47.530
when people just actually can
look each other in the eye

01:19:47.530 --> 01:19:49.330
and not pretend that they have

01:19:49.330 --> 01:19:51.090
every answer to every question.

01:19:51.090 --> 01:19:53.300
I think that's a deeply powerful thing.

01:19:53.300 --> 01:19:56.752
So when Jordan would go
onstage and he, I kid you not,

01:19:56.752 --> 01:20:00.060
it's almost unimaginable but
the guy 110 some-odd shows

01:20:00.060 --> 01:20:03.170
that we did together, he never
gave the same lecture twice.

01:20:03.170 --> 01:20:04.003
He would give a different,

01:20:04.003 --> 01:20:06.550
some nights he would
talk about all 12 rules,

01:20:06.550 --> 01:20:07.910
some nights he would talk about one rule,

01:20:07.910 --> 01:20:08.800
some nights four rules,

01:20:08.800 --> 01:20:10.240
some nights he would just
talk about the media,

01:20:10.240 --> 01:20:11.420
some nights he would talk about YouTube.

01:20:11.420 --> 01:20:14.490
I mean, it was just
whatever was in his mind.

01:20:14.490 --> 01:20:16.220
And you know, the book,
he was on a book tour

01:20:16.220 --> 01:20:17.060
they wanted him to sell a book.

01:20:17.060 --> 01:20:18.130
Sometimes the book never came up.

01:20:18.130 --> 01:20:21.330
I mean, he just didn't he,

01:20:21.330 --> 01:20:23.220
but to answer your question specifically

01:20:23.220 --> 01:20:26.153
he was being present as much as possible.

01:20:27.010 --> 01:20:30.160
That is something that I
really try to do in my life

01:20:30.160 --> 01:20:32.710
and when you're doing it right
you don't really have to try

01:20:32.710 --> 01:20:34.550
because it just starts happening

01:20:34.550 --> 01:20:36.180
and you just start being real.

01:20:36.180 --> 01:20:37.790
And that's why actually I've thoroughly,

01:20:37.790 --> 01:20:40.060
as I just said to you guys at halftime

01:20:40.060 --> 01:20:42.710
or whatever we're calling
it the Dave Rubin grilling.

01:20:45.479 --> 01:20:48.990
I've really enjoyed this because
this is real, this is real.

01:20:48.990 --> 01:20:50.996
And the more that we can do that,

01:20:50.996 --> 01:20:53.090
the better we can understand each other.

01:20:53.090 --> 01:20:55.070
So there would be nights
where he would be on stage,

01:20:55.070 --> 01:20:56.040
you know, telling the story

01:20:56.040 --> 01:20:57.080
that I'm sure many of you have heard

01:20:57.080 --> 01:20:58.370
where he talks about Pinocchio.

01:20:58.370 --> 01:21:00.810
And the reason that you
have to wish upon a star

01:21:00.810 --> 01:21:02.345
and what that means and
then he would link it

01:21:02.345 --> 01:21:06.770
to a biblical story and he would tear up

01:21:06.770 --> 01:21:08.170
and he would cry and
you could hear the crack

01:21:08.170 --> 01:21:10.820
in his voice and it was real.

01:21:10.820 --> 01:21:12.930
We would be backstage
right after the show,

01:21:12.930 --> 01:21:13.860
just sitting in the greenroom

01:21:13.860 --> 01:21:16.180
and I would just kind of sit there

01:21:16.180 --> 01:21:18.200
and give him his moment
to collect himself.

01:21:18.200 --> 01:21:19.050
So it was real.

01:21:19.050 --> 01:21:23.450
And I think all that really
is is being vulnerable,

01:21:23.450 --> 01:21:27.520
being open, the best way
I could describe it was

01:21:27.520 --> 01:21:30.080
what I saw the guy do was that every night

01:21:30.080 --> 01:21:32.150
that he went onstage he tried to take

01:21:32.150 --> 01:21:35.330
as much of his intellect
and his set of beliefs

01:21:35.330 --> 01:21:38.100
he would take them as far as
he could on any given night

01:21:38.100 --> 01:21:38.933
and there were nights

01:21:38.933 --> 01:21:41.640
where I could see him stretch
that a little bit further.

01:21:41.640 --> 01:21:43.940
That's a pretty incredible
thing to be around.

01:21:45.540 --> 01:21:47.640
- On a slightly lighter note, someone ask,

01:21:48.710 --> 01:21:51.380
Jordan Peterson versus Ben
Shapiro wrestling match,

01:21:51.380 --> 01:21:52.613
Who'd win?

01:21:52.613 --> 01:21:54.780
(audience laughing)

01:21:54.780 --> 01:21:58.843
- Well Jordan's tall and
lanky Ben is quick and short.

01:22:01.998 --> 01:22:05.220
So I feel like he'd take
out the legs, you know?

01:22:05.220 --> 01:22:07.660
Ben probably, probably, Ben will start,

01:22:07.660 --> 01:22:09.564
Ben's you know about, you
know, 20 years younger, so.

01:22:09.564 --> 01:22:13.123
- So many of these
questions actually are--

01:22:13.123 --> 01:22:13.956
- He could actually use the yarmulke

01:22:13.956 --> 01:22:16.240
as some kind of boomerang or something.

01:22:16.240 --> 01:22:18.493
There's things, there's
things the guy can do.

01:22:22.547 --> 01:22:23.670
- Here's a serious question.

01:22:23.670 --> 01:22:25.478
- What am I doing here, exactly?

01:22:25.478 --> 01:22:28.240
- This is really, this is a tough one.

01:22:28.240 --> 01:22:32.240
I mean, I know I've heard
this you put this position

01:22:33.101 --> 01:22:34.410
but as much as you
obviously have a great deal

01:22:34.410 --> 01:22:36.460
of respect for people like Ben Shapiro

01:22:36.460 --> 01:22:37.907
and other religious figures,

01:22:37.907 --> 01:22:39.100
you disagree with them

01:22:39.100 --> 01:22:40.990
on some pretty fundamental things,

01:22:40.990 --> 01:22:41.901
some ethical issues, for instance.

01:22:41.901 --> 01:22:42.734
- Sure.

01:22:42.734 --> 01:22:45.090
- So, for instance, Ben
Shapiro's very strongly opposed

01:22:45.090 --> 01:22:48.520
to abortion and someone
asks you on text here

01:22:48.520 --> 01:22:52.380
what about you, what would it
take to change your position

01:22:52.380 --> 01:22:53.590
on that particular ethical issue

01:22:53.590 --> 01:22:55.890
knowing that you are pro-choice?

01:22:55.890 --> 01:22:59.170
- So I describe myself as
begrudgingly pro-choice

01:22:59.170 --> 01:23:03.470
which I take no pleasure in
even discussing abortion.

01:23:03.470 --> 01:23:05.430
I find this to be the issue,

01:23:05.430 --> 01:23:07.280
beyond any other political issue,

01:23:07.280 --> 01:23:09.540
I find this to be the most polarizing

01:23:09.540 --> 01:23:10.613
and almost impossible to talk about

01:23:10.613 --> 01:23:12.310
because you're talking about

01:23:13.500 --> 01:23:14.980
the most existential question there is

01:23:14.980 --> 01:23:16.460
which is the beginning of life, right?

01:23:16.460 --> 01:23:18.710
And the way that it gets
framed, unfortunately,

01:23:18.710 --> 01:23:21.270
through the media is that
if you're on the right

01:23:21.270 --> 01:23:23.340
you somehow hate women
and if you're on the left

01:23:23.340 --> 01:23:24.420
you somehow hate babies.

01:23:24.420 --> 01:23:27.560
And that sort of ridiculous false choice

01:23:27.560 --> 01:23:28.810
that we're constantly,

01:23:28.810 --> 01:23:32.060
basically playing ping-pong
between is insane.

01:23:32.060 --> 01:23:35.510
My position basically is that,

01:23:35.510 --> 01:23:38.550
look, I believe in the individual.

01:23:38.550 --> 01:23:40.500
Now I get why that could be messy

01:23:40.500 --> 01:23:41.850
in a conversation about abortion

01:23:41.850 --> 01:23:44.960
because I do believe that
a fetus is life, I do.

01:23:44.960 --> 01:23:48.320
And we can, and it's up to
ethicists and philosophers

01:23:48.320 --> 01:23:52.760
and all sorts of scientific minded people

01:23:52.760 --> 01:23:54.650
and theologians actually, as well,

01:23:54.650 --> 01:23:56.860
to decide is that the moment
the sperm meets the egg

01:23:56.860 --> 01:23:58.060
or is that two weeks later?

01:23:58.060 --> 01:23:59.780
And there's different religious traditions

01:23:59.780 --> 01:24:02.140
that can teach you all
sorts of different things.

01:24:02.140 --> 01:24:05.340
My belief is that the individual,

01:24:05.340 --> 01:24:09.240
we as a society cannot
have a perfect system

01:24:09.240 --> 01:24:12.030
that is perfect for everyone all the time.

01:24:12.030 --> 01:24:15.900
What we can try to do is
have some set of rules

01:24:15.900 --> 01:24:19.293
that can maximize human
flourishing the most it can.

01:24:20.228 --> 01:24:22.410
What I believe is basically

01:24:22.410 --> 01:24:24.210
that you have a couple week window.

01:24:24.210 --> 01:24:26.900
A few years ago and the
first time I debated this

01:24:26.900 --> 01:24:29.930
with Ben Shapiro who's
obviously completely pro-life,

01:24:29.930 --> 01:24:31.370
so he's completely anti-abortion,

01:24:31.370 --> 01:24:33.910
I used to say 20 weeks because
there was scientific evidence

01:24:33.910 --> 01:24:36.200
that at 20 weeks the fetus
can actually feel pain.

01:24:36.200 --> 01:24:37.210
And what Ben said to me,

01:24:37.210 --> 01:24:38.917
which I think is a completely
legitimate argument, he said,

01:24:38.917 --> 01:24:41.477
"Well, so you're saying
you're admitting it's a life

01:24:41.477 --> 01:24:43.287
"and you're acknowledging,
well, if you're saying 20 weeks,

01:24:43.287 --> 01:24:45.023
"well then 18 weeks is
obviously still alive.

01:24:45.023 --> 01:24:46.290
"Why not 18 weeks?"

01:24:46.290 --> 01:24:50.150
And I granted him that
inconsistency in this view

01:24:50.150 --> 01:24:52.790
because I'm not trying to
come up with a perfect system.

01:24:52.790 --> 01:24:54.470
I'm trying to come up with some system

01:24:54.470 --> 01:24:57.930
that would allow the 350
million people that live

01:24:57.930 --> 01:25:00.880
in this country to have some guideline

01:25:00.880 --> 01:25:04.120
that we could most, somewhat
agree on, something like that.

01:25:04.120 --> 01:25:06.560
The problem right now is

01:25:06.560 --> 01:25:08.890
that the 12 Democratic
candidates have gone

01:25:08.890 --> 01:25:12.010
so off the deep end with this
that they're talking about,

01:25:12.010 --> 01:25:14.440
I mean they're literally talking
about post-birth abortions

01:25:14.440 --> 01:25:18.130
like we have heard some
beyond hysterical crazy stuff.

01:25:18.130 --> 01:25:20.000
Tulsi Gabbard, who I had on my show,

01:25:20.000 --> 01:25:22.490
she said that she would not
want third term abortions

01:25:22.490 --> 01:25:25.670
which by any measure, even
if it's not for you guys,

01:25:25.670 --> 01:25:27.990
let's say here, because I'm
assuming you're mostly pro-life

01:25:27.990 --> 01:25:32.290
that would have been considered
a pretty moderate position

01:25:32.290 --> 01:25:34.250
for a Democrat 10 years ago.

01:25:34.250 --> 01:25:36.260
But for her now in that field

01:25:36.260 --> 01:25:39.130
that is a wildly out there position

01:25:39.130 --> 01:25:40.900
because they all believe basically

01:25:40.900 --> 01:25:42.410
you can have an abortion, at any time.

01:25:42.410 --> 01:25:43.560
I mean this even where Biden,

01:25:43.560 --> 01:25:44.610
who's supposed to be the adult

01:25:44.610 --> 01:25:46.780
in the group is now kind of, right?

01:25:46.780 --> 01:25:47.640
He's supposed, I mean that's why

01:25:47.640 --> 01:25:48.790
they dragged him back into this,

01:25:48.790 --> 01:25:50.667
he didn't want to do it they're, you know,

01:25:50.667 --> 01:25:52.490
but he was supposed to be the adult

01:25:52.490 --> 01:25:53.990
and go guys know you're all bananas

01:25:53.990 --> 01:25:55.120
and you know settle down.

01:25:55.120 --> 01:25:57.220
But it's not working.

01:25:57.220 --> 01:25:59.500
So what was your question?

01:25:59.500 --> 01:26:00.736
What would get me to change on that or?

01:26:00.736 --> 01:26:01.569
- Well I suppose that

01:26:01.569 --> 01:26:03.434
that would be a really
interesting thing to ask.

01:26:03.434 --> 01:26:06.357
Would this journey that you're going on

01:26:06.357 --> 01:26:11.010
of being open to potentially
God and to religious answers

01:26:11.010 --> 01:26:13.520
to things cause you to
reconsider your view on that.

01:26:13.520 --> 01:26:14.353
- Well, I would say these things

01:26:14.353 --> 01:26:16.916
don't have nothing to do
with each other, right?

01:26:16.916 --> 01:26:21.020
So if we find, if we find a
speck of something on the moon

01:26:21.020 --> 01:26:23.980
that has some resemblance
of life or a cell

01:26:23.980 --> 01:26:25.600
or something we say it's life.

01:26:25.600 --> 01:26:29.330
So I'm not denying that from conception.

01:26:29.330 --> 01:26:30.640
I'm actually not denying that's life.

01:26:30.640 --> 01:26:32.700
What I'm trying to do just as someone

01:26:32.700 --> 01:26:37.700
that talks about things
in a public way is to,

01:26:37.890 --> 01:26:39.870
you're not gonna satisfy
everybody all the time.

01:26:39.870 --> 01:26:43.110
And by the way the most
uncomfortable truth about this is

01:26:43.110 --> 01:26:45.780
that humans get left with
all sorts of horrific choices

01:26:45.780 --> 01:26:47.460
that they don't want to have to deal with.

01:26:47.460 --> 01:26:49.540
And if abortion was completely illegal

01:26:49.540 --> 01:26:51.220
that actually would not stop abortion.

01:26:51.220 --> 01:26:53.030
It would also make it much more dangerous.

01:26:53.030 --> 01:26:54.360
There would be access for people

01:26:54.360 --> 01:26:56.200
that had certain resources financially,

01:26:56.200 --> 01:26:57.230
that other people wouldn't have.

01:26:57.230 --> 01:26:59.430
- [Justin] It's very complex, yes, yes.

01:26:59.430 --> 01:27:01.640
- There's just a series
of uncomfortable truths

01:27:01.640 --> 01:27:04.570
that we would have to
deal with to do that.

01:27:04.570 --> 01:27:07.280
But I would say I would say
I'm begrudgingly pro-choice

01:27:07.280 --> 01:27:12.010
and trust me this one
more than a God question

01:27:12.010 --> 01:27:14.100
because this a sort of policy issue.

01:27:14.100 --> 01:27:15.110
I mean up here and now,

01:27:15.110 --> 01:27:18.720
I find it to be the most
difficult one to answer.

01:27:18.720 --> 01:27:20.250
- [Justin] I mean John,
I'd just interested

01:27:20.250 --> 01:27:21.510
in your reflection on this,

01:27:21.510 --> 01:27:23.820
not necessarily getting to
the bottom of this issue

01:27:23.820 --> 01:27:25.560
which could take us all evening

01:27:25.560 --> 01:27:30.020
but to what extent for you
does being a believer in God

01:27:30.020 --> 01:27:32.353
and specifically Christianity mean

01:27:32.353 --> 01:27:34.070
that your views on that kind of thing have

01:27:34.070 --> 01:27:36.700
to take a certain direction in terms

01:27:36.700 --> 01:27:39.970
of the views of abortion
and the sanctity of life

01:27:39.970 --> 01:27:41.740
and that kind of thing?

01:27:41.740 --> 01:27:45.020
- I think the important thing to realize,

01:27:45.020 --> 01:27:48.010
and we don't focus it sharply enough,

01:27:48.010 --> 01:27:52.023
is that in part ethics
are worldview dependent.

01:27:52.930 --> 01:27:55.150
What I mean by that is this.

01:27:55.150 --> 01:27:59.140
If you think that a fetus is
just simply at the early stage

01:27:59.140 --> 01:28:02.500
an undifferentiated block of cells,

01:28:02.500 --> 01:28:05.333
it's only matter, why not deal with it?

01:28:06.260 --> 01:28:10.940
As has been put to me by a
world-class gynecologist.

01:28:10.940 --> 01:28:13.137
And I pointed out to her, I said,

01:28:13.137 --> 01:28:17.550
"But you come to that
because of your atheism."

01:28:17.550 --> 01:28:20.933
Now, I said to her, "What would you do?

01:28:22.127 --> 01:28:26.787
"That's not only life after
conception, it's human life."

01:28:28.090 --> 01:28:31.720
From where I sit it
bears the image of God,

01:28:31.720 --> 01:28:33.363
what right have I to stop it?

01:28:34.860 --> 01:28:39.260
So that the ethics are
worldview dependent.

01:28:39.260 --> 01:28:42.160
And this is one of the huge problems,

01:28:42.160 --> 01:28:44.560
way beyond that question.

01:28:44.560 --> 01:28:47.600
You know, if we teach kids in school

01:28:48.660 --> 01:28:51.560
that they are purely animals, full stop,

01:28:51.560 --> 01:28:53.677
we'll see they start
to behave like animals.

01:28:53.677 --> 01:28:56.230
And that's exactly what
we see in our society.

01:28:56.230 --> 01:29:00.010
Knife crime in London has
just gone off the map.

01:29:00.010 --> 01:29:03.350
But it's because people have been taught.

01:29:03.350 --> 01:29:07.220
And you were talking about abortion,

01:29:07.220 --> 01:29:09.090
these killing infants after birth

01:29:09.090 --> 01:29:12.010
that's Peter Singer's
view and I've debated him.

01:29:12.010 --> 01:29:15.730
And the issue stems
straight from his atheism.

01:29:15.730 --> 01:29:18.490
And so I think we ought to be more upfront

01:29:18.490 --> 01:29:23.490
about the connection
between some of these issues

01:29:23.680 --> 01:29:26.830
and the worldview of the society around.

01:29:26.830 --> 01:29:28.640
- If I could just add on that quickly.

01:29:28.640 --> 01:29:29.970
I mentioned this equality forum

01:29:29.970 --> 01:29:31.310
that the Democrats had last night.

01:29:31.310 --> 01:29:33.950
And one of the things
that, it seems to me that

01:29:33.950 --> 01:29:35.530
they're always trying to outdo themselves

01:29:35.530 --> 01:29:38.310
and who can be the most
Left or the most progressive

01:29:38.310 --> 01:29:39.400
or the most collectivist.

01:29:39.400 --> 01:29:40.810
And you may have heard this last night

01:29:40.810 --> 01:29:43.173
but Beto O'Rourke basically said,

01:29:43.173 --> 01:29:44.489
the churches and places of worship

01:29:44.489 --> 01:29:46.590
that aren't for same-sex marriage,

01:29:46.590 --> 01:29:49.240
he would want them to
lose their tax exemption.

01:29:49.240 --> 01:29:53.440
That's an absolutely absurd position.

01:29:53.440 --> 01:29:56.790
But this why those of us, we can disagree

01:29:56.790 --> 01:29:58.970
on these sort of margin issues

01:29:58.970 --> 01:30:01.590
but we have to come together
around the freedom issue,

01:30:01.590 --> 01:30:05.610
number one because will come next is that

01:30:05.610 --> 01:30:06.590
they'll say all right, well now

01:30:06.590 --> 01:30:08.850
if you're not completely pro-choice

01:30:08.850 --> 01:30:10.180
you should lose your tax exemption.

01:30:10.180 --> 01:30:12.220
And they will just keep encroaching

01:30:12.220 --> 01:30:13.520
on every set of beliefs you have.

01:30:13.520 --> 01:30:16.280
So I may not share that
exact belief with you

01:30:16.280 --> 01:30:20.570
but I would 100% defend your
ability to have that belief

01:30:20.570 --> 01:30:23.476
in the place that you
worship without question.

01:30:23.476 --> 01:30:26.726
(audience applauding)

01:30:28.340 --> 01:30:30.000
- Got one for you here, John.

01:30:30.000 --> 01:30:33.940
And it's keeping on a sort of
pretty serious tack as well.

01:30:33.940 --> 01:30:35.350
This person asks on the premise

01:30:35.350 --> 01:30:37.420
that we are created in the image of God

01:30:37.420 --> 01:30:39.710
and we each have value how do you respond

01:30:39.710 --> 01:30:44.070
to the growing rate of
suicide in the West?

01:30:44.070 --> 01:30:46.620
I think we're seeing
this especially among men

01:30:46.620 --> 01:30:51.620
that suicide is increasing
at an alarming rate.

01:30:53.000 --> 01:30:57.410
Where do you see that coming
from and how do you speak to it

01:30:57.410 --> 01:30:58.760
as someone who does
believe people are made

01:30:58.760 --> 01:30:59.681
in the image of God?

01:30:59.681 --> 01:31:03.470
what does suicide have to,
what's the response I suppose,

01:31:03.470 --> 01:31:05.970
to somebody who goes that route?

01:31:05.970 --> 01:31:08.220
This is very complicated question

01:31:08.220 --> 01:31:10.830
because suicides are all different.

01:31:10.830 --> 01:31:11.663
I have a colleague

01:31:11.663 --> 01:31:14.183
who's one of the world's
experts on suicide.

01:31:15.070 --> 01:31:17.200
And just talking to him makes me realize

01:31:17.200 --> 01:31:20.530
that this a very complex thing.

01:31:20.530 --> 01:31:23.493
But just thinking about it simplistically,

01:31:24.750 --> 01:31:26.350
people tend to commit suicide

01:31:26.350 --> 01:31:30.070
when they have nothing to live for,

01:31:30.070 --> 01:31:33.353
they've got no future that they can see.

01:31:34.620 --> 01:31:38.990
And I do believe that the
Christian message addresses

01:31:38.990 --> 01:31:42.917
that head-on by giving people that.

01:31:42.917 --> 01:31:45.470
And I have personally seen people come

01:31:45.470 --> 01:31:47.250
from the edge of suicide

01:31:49.020 --> 01:31:52.240
and become settled,
balanced everything else

01:31:52.240 --> 01:31:54.117
because they've come to faith in Christ.

01:31:54.117 --> 01:31:56.170
And they've got that meaning

01:31:56.170 --> 01:32:01.053
that has answered that
need to destroy themselves.

01:32:02.515 --> 01:32:06.120
And the difficulty is, I'm too old now

01:32:06.120 --> 01:32:09.770
to really understand the vicious effect

01:32:10.640 --> 01:32:14.310
that Facebook for example,
likes and dislikes,

01:32:14.310 --> 01:32:19.287
have on young people where
they get bullied in cyberspace

01:32:20.649 --> 01:32:23.470
and they feel completely awful

01:32:23.470 --> 01:32:26.340
and they lose all their self-worth.

01:32:26.340 --> 01:32:29.330
I mean the cruelty that people exhibit

01:32:29.330 --> 01:32:32.330
to one another is unbelievable.

01:32:32.330 --> 01:32:36.490
And what's the answer to that?

01:32:36.490 --> 01:32:40.080
Well the answer, I've
got to start with me.

01:32:40.080 --> 01:32:42.447
I love Jordan's thing,
"Clean up your own room

01:32:42.447 --> 01:32:44.760
"before you clean up the world."

01:32:44.760 --> 01:32:48.280
And it needs to start with
me, the people I know,

01:32:48.280 --> 01:32:50.870
looking for the signs of depression

01:32:50.870 --> 01:32:54.730
or feeling down or feeling
lonely or feeling meaningless.

01:32:54.730 --> 01:32:57.510
We can all do a little something.

01:32:57.510 --> 01:33:00.610
It may not be much, it
may not hit the headlines

01:33:00.610 --> 01:33:04.800
but it could take someone
back from the brink.

01:33:04.800 --> 01:33:08.650
There's no universal answer to it.

01:33:08.650 --> 01:33:11.130
- There is a follow-up
question that this has sparked

01:33:11.130 --> 01:33:14.220
and has been coming in
which is what about the case

01:33:14.220 --> 01:33:16.627
of believing Christians
who perhaps commit suicide.

01:33:16.627 --> 01:33:19.410
And I think that has been
quite a significant case here

01:33:19.410 --> 01:33:21.250
in Southern California recently.

01:33:21.250 --> 01:33:24.230
- Well I know of one or
two very significant cases

01:33:24.230 --> 01:33:26.050
of people I actually knew.

01:33:28.419 --> 01:33:32.610
And I'm not a doctor,
I'm not a psychologist

01:33:32.610 --> 01:33:34.540
but we've got to realize

01:33:34.540 --> 01:33:38.773
that our minds can actually break down.

01:33:40.380 --> 01:33:43.280
There used to be a time,
fortunately it's past,

01:33:43.280 --> 01:33:47.180
where psychiatrists and
psychologists were looked upon

01:33:47.180 --> 01:33:50.313
by many in the Christian
community with a lot of suspicion.

01:33:51.260 --> 01:33:53.560
If you broke your leg,
you went to a doctor

01:33:53.560 --> 01:33:56.290
and that was fine, everybody
thought that was fine.

01:33:56.290 --> 01:34:00.628
But if your mind broke that wasn't fine.

01:34:00.628 --> 01:34:02.240
And I think we've got to realize

01:34:02.240 --> 01:34:05.833
that even to believing
Christians, they're still human.

01:34:07.020 --> 01:34:10.760
They still have got
flaws and difficulties.

01:34:10.760 --> 01:34:12.950
Your temperament doesn't change overnight,

01:34:12.950 --> 01:34:15.450
you may have noticed, when
you become a Christian.

01:34:16.321 --> 01:34:17.460
And if you haven't noticed that

01:34:17.460 --> 01:34:19.334
you ought to wake up and notice it.

01:34:19.334 --> 01:34:22.992
(audience laughing)

01:34:22.992 --> 01:34:26.060
And therefore we can break down,

01:34:26.060 --> 01:34:29.460
the chemical imbalance in our brain can go

01:34:30.890 --> 01:34:35.213
and people jump off a
cliff or shoot themselves.

01:34:36.750 --> 01:34:40.100
We need to be very careful
how we judge those people

01:34:41.010 --> 01:34:45.400
because we don't see inside their mind.

01:34:45.400 --> 01:34:50.400
And I want to say, okay this has happened,

01:34:50.490 --> 01:34:55.490
it's tragic but don't judge
what was going on in their mind.

01:34:56.610 --> 01:34:59.948
You've got to leave it to God ultimately.

01:34:59.948 --> 01:35:02.113
(audience applauding)

01:35:02.113 --> 01:35:04.196
- Can I just add to that?

01:35:06.030 --> 01:35:07.730
I would just add one thing to that quickly

01:35:07.730 --> 01:35:09.520
which is there's a
strange paradox right now

01:35:09.520 --> 01:35:11.920
because we're more connected
than we've ever been, right?

01:35:11.920 --> 01:35:13.480
We're all on Twitter
and Facebook and YouTube

01:35:13.480 --> 01:35:15.077
and all these things and
we can connect with people.

01:35:15.077 --> 01:35:16.670
And we can send pictures to people

01:35:16.670 --> 01:35:18.070
and do all this stuff, right?

01:35:18.070 --> 01:35:20.050
And it feels very connected.

01:35:20.050 --> 01:35:23.380
And then on the other hand
the number one type of email

01:35:23.380 --> 01:35:27.770
that I get is people that are
afraid to say what they think

01:35:27.770 --> 01:35:29.720
and they say they watch my show

01:35:29.720 --> 01:35:31.290
because they're afraid
to say what they think.

01:35:31.290 --> 01:35:33.850
And I happen to be having
some of the conversations

01:35:33.850 --> 01:35:36.330
that are the things that
they're thinking about.

01:35:36.330 --> 01:35:39.220
And I suspect that if
you're walking around,

01:35:39.220 --> 01:35:41.580
as most of us do probably everyday,

01:35:41.580 --> 01:35:44.090
not really saying the
things that you're thinking

01:35:45.400 --> 01:35:46.880
the end result of that

01:35:46.880 --> 01:35:49.900
will be such a disconnect from reality

01:35:49.900 --> 01:35:52.390
because all you've got
then left is your own mind.

01:35:52.390 --> 01:35:55.950
And if you do that over and
over and over and over again

01:35:55.950 --> 01:35:58.870
you got nothing left and I suspect

01:35:58.870 --> 01:36:00.700
that would be the type of place

01:36:00.700 --> 01:36:04.930
where there would be you could
see yourself having to make

01:36:04.930 --> 01:36:06.995
that horrific choice because you're so--

01:36:06.995 --> 01:36:08.750
- Connected versus not friendship&gt;

01:36:08.750 --> 01:36:09.583
- [Dave] Yeah.

01:36:11.064 --> 01:36:13.700
- Yeah, absolutely, we've gone over

01:36:13.700 --> 01:36:16.270
some pretty big issues tonight

01:36:16.270 --> 01:36:18.340
and we've had many, many more questions in

01:36:18.340 --> 01:36:20.193
than we can answer.

01:36:21.270 --> 01:36:24.530
There's just one maybe which
kind of sums up the evening

01:36:24.530 --> 01:36:26.900
to some extent that we've had

01:36:26.900 --> 01:36:31.520
which is a question for
John referring today

01:36:31.520 --> 01:36:35.163
which is, John, do you want
Dave to become a Christian?

01:36:35.163 --> 01:36:38.163
(audience laughing)

01:36:42.720 --> 01:36:46.628
- I really have to ask my agent
what the original pitch was.

01:36:46.628 --> 01:36:48.025
(audience laughing)

01:36:48.025 --> 01:36:49.630
- I don't choose, well,

01:36:49.630 --> 01:36:51.140
the questions kind of
choose themselves here.

01:36:51.140 --> 01:36:53.493
There so many people are basically asking,

01:36:54.594 --> 01:36:55.810
hey, what, can you ask Dave

01:36:55.810 --> 01:36:57.320
this question about Christianity.

01:36:57.320 --> 01:36:59.420
So I had very little choice in the matter.

01:36:59.420 --> 01:37:03.023
But yeah, I mean, I'm guessing
the answer is yes, John.

01:37:03.023 --> 01:37:04.212
- You're not guessing.

01:37:04.212 --> 01:37:07.212
(audience laughing)

01:37:08.600 --> 01:37:13.600
Because I've experienced a
life which I don't deserve,

01:37:15.190 --> 01:37:18.020
which has been full of profound joy,

01:37:18.020 --> 01:37:23.020
a journey that has been
deeply meaningful to me

01:37:23.447 --> 01:37:27.063
and it's connected with my
relationship with Christ,

01:37:28.430 --> 01:37:33.020
of course not only Dave but everybody here

01:37:33.020 --> 01:37:34.877
because there may be many skeptics here

01:37:34.877 --> 01:37:37.170
and people watching online.

01:37:37.170 --> 01:37:42.080
Of course, my desire
is that they can share

01:37:42.080 --> 01:37:44.750
that experience that I
share and become Christians.

01:37:44.750 --> 01:37:49.750
But, but, I'm not in the business

01:37:50.130 --> 01:37:51.810
of browbeating people

01:37:52.970 --> 01:37:57.970
and forcing them beyond where they are.

01:37:58.040 --> 01:38:00.670
I feel very strongly

01:38:00.670 --> 01:38:03.730
and that's why I believe
in public discussion.

01:38:03.730 --> 01:38:06.020
Let's have the discussion on the table

01:38:06.020 --> 01:38:10.080
from different points of
view but trust the people

01:38:10.080 --> 01:38:11.773
to make up their own mind.

01:38:12.640 --> 01:38:17.640
And so my motivation is
to get the message out

01:38:20.340 --> 01:38:22.730
and leave the results with God.

01:38:22.730 --> 01:38:25.960
But my desire is that,
of course it's that.

01:38:25.960 --> 01:38:30.960
But don't specify Dave
alone poor chap. (laughs)

01:38:31.090 --> 01:38:31.990
- Thank you, John.

01:38:34.537 --> 01:38:37.463
- Just think of our
neighbors and our friends.

01:38:40.070 --> 01:38:42.290
Is it our desire?

01:38:42.290 --> 01:38:45.080
I think, when I'm asked
a question like this

01:38:45.080 --> 01:38:47.450
I get very slightly nervous

01:38:47.450 --> 01:38:52.450
because I want to relate
to people as whole persons

01:38:52.660 --> 01:38:56.023
not just as evangelism fodder.

01:38:57.290 --> 01:38:59.713
And that's very important.

01:38:59.713 --> 01:39:02.880
(audience applauding)

01:39:05.220 --> 01:39:07.770
Dave, you have been a
tremendous sport tonight

01:39:07.770 --> 01:39:09.620
and I can only say,
you've been so gracious

01:39:09.620 --> 01:39:11.590
and willing to engage with you know--

01:39:11.590 --> 01:39:13.012
- Exactly.

01:39:13.012 --> 01:39:14.850
- Occasionally being
browbeaten actually on this.

01:39:14.850 --> 01:39:19.370
But I guess, you know, if we come

01:39:19.370 --> 01:39:20.330
to just some final thoughts

01:39:20.330 --> 01:39:22.700
at the end of our conversation now,

01:39:22.700 --> 01:39:23.830
what for you has been the value

01:39:23.830 --> 01:39:25.820
of having a conversation like this?

01:39:25.820 --> 01:39:28.940
What for you, why do you sit
down, week in and week out

01:39:28.940 --> 01:39:31.240
with people that you may
not necessarily agree with

01:39:31.240 --> 01:39:32.850
on everything but you're interested

01:39:32.850 --> 01:39:33.683
in finding stuff out from?

01:39:33.683 --> 01:39:35.460
What's the value of these kind

01:39:35.460 --> 01:39:37.450
of big conversations around culture, faith

01:39:37.450 --> 01:39:39.360
and God in the end?

01:39:39.360 --> 01:39:41.900
- Yeah, well first off, it
never struck me as so obvious.

01:39:41.900 --> 01:39:44.560
You know, Jews don't
try to convert people.

01:39:44.560 --> 01:39:47.053
That's made this doubly
funny for me, I think.

01:39:48.070 --> 01:39:50.620
No, first off, just joking aside.

01:39:50.620 --> 01:39:51.870
This has been an absolute pleasure.

01:39:51.870 --> 01:39:55.410
You know, I have found that
when I am having an interview

01:39:55.410 --> 01:39:57.200
with someone that is really real,

01:39:57.200 --> 01:40:01.410
when we're suddenly hitting
some unchartered territory

01:40:01.410 --> 01:40:04.830
or I can really see somebody
exploring a new idea

01:40:04.830 --> 01:40:07.730
or not giving me the packaged answer

01:40:07.730 --> 01:40:10.710
and we've really
connected in that deep way

01:40:10.710 --> 01:40:12.993
that I can actually feel something.

01:40:12.993 --> 01:40:13.826
And I really mean that.

01:40:13.826 --> 01:40:18.040
I can feel a physical
reaction to that sort of warm,

01:40:18.040 --> 01:40:19.439
I can't really fully describe it.

01:40:19.439 --> 01:40:20.970
But it's not just something physical.

01:40:20.970 --> 01:40:23.420
It's almost like, truly, it's almost like

01:40:23.420 --> 01:40:26.720
I can see almost like
an aura around somebody.

01:40:26.720 --> 01:40:29.630
And so this sort of a religious something

01:40:29.630 --> 01:40:30.463
I guess I'm giving you.

01:40:30.463 --> 01:40:33.000
But I can feel something
when something is truly real

01:40:33.000 --> 01:40:34.460
and I think we all know those moments

01:40:34.460 --> 01:40:37.420
when you sort of let all
of the other stuff go

01:40:37.420 --> 01:40:38.930
and you're just having
something that's real.

01:40:38.930 --> 01:40:40.610
There's something there.

01:40:40.610 --> 01:40:43.140
However, you want to describe that.

01:40:43.140 --> 01:40:46.810
I love being on this type of adventure,

01:40:46.810 --> 01:40:48.400
this type of conversation,

01:40:48.400 --> 01:40:51.040
this is exactly why I do what I do

01:40:52.200 --> 01:40:54.600
because we desperately need it.

01:40:54.600 --> 01:40:56.790
You know, there's been a, I think theme

01:40:56.790 --> 01:40:59.960
throughout all of this beyond
the personal stuff to me

01:40:59.960 --> 01:41:02.130
but I think theme really was

01:41:02.130 --> 01:41:03.990
that there is a crisis of meaning

01:41:03.990 --> 01:41:06.750
and we're all sort of realizing

01:41:06.750 --> 01:41:09.220
that the world is in
complete flux right now.

01:41:09.220 --> 01:41:11.260
The future feels like it's in flux.

01:41:11.260 --> 01:41:13.390
I'm 43 years old I was born in '76.

01:41:13.390 --> 01:41:14.690
At no point in my life,

01:41:14.690 --> 01:41:17.540
until the last say two
years or three years,

01:41:17.540 --> 01:41:19.990
did the world feel like it was in flux

01:41:19.990 --> 01:41:22.490
like the future is actually confused.

01:41:22.490 --> 01:41:25.260
Will America continue to
exist the way it exists?

01:41:25.260 --> 01:41:28.160
Will the West continue to
exist the way it exists?

01:41:28.160 --> 01:41:30.050
The UK, I mean, you guys have all sorts

01:41:30.050 --> 01:41:31.259
of crazy things going on.
- Oh yeah,

01:41:31.259 --> 01:41:34.030
it's an interesting adventures for us too.

01:41:34.030 --> 01:41:36.670
- Yeah, I mean I could
do an hour on Brexit.

01:41:36.670 --> 01:41:38.230
We'll let that be.

01:41:38.230 --> 01:41:40.036
That's more difficult than God.

01:41:40.036 --> 01:41:43.460
(audience laughing)
- True.

01:41:43.460 --> 01:41:47.430
- So many people are feeling that though

01:41:47.430 --> 01:41:49.590
that sense of uncertainty.

01:41:49.590 --> 01:41:52.250
And for the final Jordan
